;;; 77-sdf.slat — Motoi Scheme Reference · Chapter 77 · library sdf
;;;
;;; 9 verbs in this library. Read chapter 00-intro.slat first.
;;;
;;; ─── THE ROW RULES (summary — full doctrine in 00-intro.slat) ───
;;;
;;;   Row 1 (HOW · Composition) — the verb written well; 1/3/5 module ladder
;;;   Row 2 (WHEN · Audit)      — operator's problem + this-verb-not-a-sibling
;;;   Row 3 (WHERE ELSE · Dim)  — same shape in 3 substrates (verb/metaphor/math)
;;;   Row 4 (WHY · Proof)       — claim + runnable test + ∀-invariant
;;;   Row 5 (WITH WHAT ELSE)    — chained verbs → named emergent pattern
;;;
;;; Every row carries the three-discipline lens: :eng · :math · :cs.
;;; Every code block RUNS through ./bin/motoi. Made by Alfred Borden.

(dialect :applies ("motoi") :note "Library sdf — 9 verbs.")

(chapter :number "77" :library "sdf" :verb-count 9 :training-eligible #t)

;; ═══ 9 verbs in library sdf ═══
;;
;; A signed distance field (SDF) is a value {sdf} carrying a function f(x,y)
;; that returns the SIGNED distance from the point (x,y) to a shape's boundary:
;; negative inside, zero on the edge, positive outside. Booleans on SDFs are
;; ARITHMETIC on those distances — union = min(a,b), intersection = max(a,b),
;; subtraction = max(a, -b). To draw, you SAMPLE the field over pixels and paint
;; where f <= 0. This is constructive solid geometry (CSG): carve complex shapes
;; from simple ones. The three constructors (circle/box/segment) return {sdf}
;; values with NO set-mode needed; the two -fill sinks PAINT and require
;; (set-mode 'sd) first.

(verb
  :name "sdf/circle" :std "Motoi"
  :library "sdf"
  :kind "meta"
  :signature "(sdf/circle cx cy r) -> {sdf}"
  :summary "Build a signed distance field for a circle of radius r centered at (cx, cy)."
  :explanation "This is the atom of the SDF layer. The value it returns is not a picture — it is a FUNCTION dressed up as a value: hand it any point (x, y) and it reports the signed distance to the circle's edge, which for a circle is simply the distance from the center minus the radius, √((x−cx)²+(y−cy)²) − r. Points inside read negative, the boundary reads zero, points outside read positive. You never see that number directly; instead you feed the field to a boolean (sdf-union, sdf-intersect, sdf-subtract) to combine it with other fields, and eventually to sdf-fill to paint every pixel where the field is ≤ 0. A circle field is the roundest, cheapest primitive in the kit and the one you reach for first when carving holes, bosses, and fillets out of boxes."
  :examples (
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "novice" :code "(sdf? (sdf/circle 100 100 30))" :note "Construct a circle field of radius 30 at the canvas center and confirm the result really is an {sdf} value — building the field paints nothing on its own.")
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "intermediate" :code "(set-mode 'sd)\n(sdf-fill (sdf/circle 160 120 40) 12)" :note "Paint the field: sample the whole 320×240 canvas and color every pixel whose signed distance is ≤ 0, producing a solid disc of radius 40.")
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "expert" :code "(define centers (list (list 80 100) (list 130 100) (list 105 70)))\n(define fields (map (lambda (c) (sdf/circle (car c) (cadr c) 26)) centers))\n(map sdf? fields)" :note "Circle fields are ordinary values, so you can build a whole list of them with map before combining any of them; every element is an independent {sdf}.")
  )
  :caveats ("Radius r should be positive; a zero or negative radius yields a field that is empty or everywhere-inside and will not paint the disc you expect." "The field is only a description — nothing appears until you pass it to sdf-fill or sdf-fill-rect after (set-mode 'sd)." "Centers are in canvas pixel coordinates (origin top-left, 320×240 at sd), not normalized 0–1 coordinates." "Distances are computed against the SD canvas resolution; changing resolution changes which pixels the same field covers.")
  :drawbacks ("A circle is the only shape it makes — for corners you need sdf/box, for strokes sdf/segment." "It cannot draw a ring by itself; a ring is a subtraction of two circle fields (see sdf-subtract)." "Sampling the whole canvas to paint one small disc is wasteful; prefer sdf-fill-rect to bound the sample region.")
  :usecases ("The base disc for a snowman, a wheel, a planet, or a button." "The hole you carve out of a plate with sdf-subtract to make a washer or a keyhole." "One term of a union that grows a blobby, organic outline from overlapping discs." "A rounded end-cap composed with sdf/box to soften a rectangle's corners.")
  :related ("sdf/box" "sdf/segment" "sdf-union" "sdf-subtract" "sdf-fill")
  :learn (:concept "A circle as a LEVEL SET of the distance function: the shape is the set of points where signed distance is ≤ 0, and the boundary is where it equals 0." :prerequisites ("set-mode" "sdf-fill") :progression "Start with one circle field; paint it; then carve a smaller circle out of it with sdf-subtract to feel how the field, not the pixels, is what you are manipulating.")
  :row-2-audit (

    (:problem "A cart author wants a round porthole window on a spaceship hull: a solid disc they can later cut into. They are tempted to reach for the raster ellipse verb, but they want the shape to combine cleanly with the rest of the hull's cutouts rather than just splat pixels."
     :program "(set-mode 'sd)\n(sdf-fill (sdf/circle 160 120 45) 12)"
     :eng-program "(set-mode 'sd)\n(define (porthole cx cy r color)\n  ;; hardened: name the field, bound the paint to its bounding box, one color knob\n  (let ((field (sdf/circle cx cy r)))\n    (sdf-fill-rect field (- cx r) (- cy r) (+ cx r) (+ cy r) color)))\n(porthole 160 120 45 12)"
     :math-program "(define (inside-circle? cx cy r x y)\n  ;; the field's SIGN is the membership test: f(x,y) <= 0 means inside-or-on\n  (<= (- (sqrt (+ (* (- x cx) (- x cx)) (* (- y cy) (- y cy)))) r) 0))\n(list (inside-circle? 100 100 30 100 100)   ;; center: inside\n      (inside-circle? 100 100 30 130 100)   ;; on the edge\n      (inside-circle? 100 100 30 200 100))  ;; far away: outside"
     :cs-program "(define (make-circle-field cx cy r)\n  (if (and (number? cx) (number? cy) (number? r) (> r 0))\n      (sdf/circle cx cy r)\n      (error 'bad-circle-args cx cy r)))\n(sdf? (make-circle-field 160 120 45))"
     :explanation "eng: name the field and paint only its bounding box so the porthole is cheap. math: the field's sign IS the point-in-circle predicate — no separate hit test needed. cs: a smart constructor that guards r > 0 before building the field."
     :meta "The raster ellipse verb paints pixels immediately and returns nothing you can cut into — you cannot subtract a hole from pixels that are already on the screen. sdf/box makes corners, not a round window. sdf/segment makes a stroke, not a filled disc. sdf/circle is the round-solid primitive precisely because its result is a reusable field, so the porthole can be carved, unioned, and re-colored downstream.")
    (:eng "The audit lens is 'did I build a field or did I paint pixels?' — the smoke test is (sdf? result): a value that answers #t can still be cut into, pixels already on screen cannot." :math "The circle is the sublevel set {(x,y) : dist((x,y),(cx,cy)) − r ≤ 0}; its boundary is the level set where the expression equals 0." :cs "A closure over (cx, cy, r) tagged as an {sdf}; O(1) to build, O(1) per point to evaluate."))
  :row-3-dimension (
    (:setup "A center, a radius, and a rule that turns any query point into 'how far past the edge am I' — negative within, zero on the rim, positive beyond."
     :rungs (
       (:domain "sdf-circle" :code "(sdf? (sdf/circle 100 100 30))")
       (:domain "geofence"   :code "(define (fence-signed-dist lat lon center-lat center-lon radius)\n  (- (sqrt (+ (* (- lat center-lat) (- lat center-lat)) (* (- lon center-lon) (- lon center-lon)))) radius))\n(list (fence-signed-dist 0 0 0 0 5) (fence-signed-dist 8 0 0 0 5))")
       (:domain "level-set"  :code "(define (f x y) (- (sqrt (+ (* x x) (* y y))) 1))\n(list 'inside (f 0 0) 'on (f 1 0) 'outside (f 3 0))"))
     :explanation "Rung 1: an SDF circle reports signed distance to a drawn rim. Rung 2: a geofence reports how far a GPS point is inside or outside a circular boundary, negative when safely within. Rung 3: the implicit function f(x,y)=√(x²+y²)−1 is exactly that same rule with center at the origin and radius 1. All three share one shape — a scalar field whose SIGN partitions the plane into inside / boundary / outside."
     :meta "The transfer works because 'circle' is really 'the level set of a radial distance function.' Any substrate that measures distance-from-a-center and compares it to a threshold — a geofence, a blast radius, a Wi-Fi coverage disc — is computing this same field. Grounds in book-of-math ch-advanced-06 (analytic-geometry) as the implicit-curve shape.")
     (:eng "Every radial-boundary substrate shares one probe: 'is the sign correct at three canonical points — center, rim, far outside?' — get those three right and the whole field is right." :math "An implicit representation: the shape is defined not by its points but by the zero-level-set of a continuous function ℝ² → ℝ." :cs "A pure function of (x,y) closed over (cx, cy, r); the same shape as any membership predicate expressed as a real-valued score thresholded at zero."))
  :row-4-proof (
    (:claim "sdf/circle is a total constructor: for any numeric center and positive radius it returns a value satisfying sdf?, and the boolean combinators preserve that."
     :program "(define a (sdf/circle 100 100 30))\n(define b (sdf/circle 100 100 15))\n(display (sdf? a)) (newline)\n(display (sdf? b)) (newline)\n(display (sdf? (sdf-union a b))) (newline)\n(display (sdf? (sdf-subtract a b)))"
     :eng-program "(define (all-fields? xs) (fold (lambda (x acc) (and acc (sdf? x))) #t xs))\n(all-fields? (map (lambda (r) (sdf/circle 100 100 r)) (list 5 10 20 40)))"
     :math-program "(define (field-of-radius r) (sdf/circle 100 100 r))\n;; the constructor is defined for every r > 0; sample the domain\n(map (lambda (r) (sdf? (field-of-radius r))) (iota 5 1))"
     :cs-program "(define (constructor-returns-field? cx cy r)\n  (let ((v (sdf/circle cx cy r)))\n    (and (sdf? v) (not (number? v)) (not (null? v)))))\n(constructor-returns-field? 100 100 30)"
     :invariant "∀ cx cy ∈ ℝ. ∀ r > 0. sdf?(sdf/circle cx cy r) = #t  ∧  sdf?(sdf-subtract (sdf/circle cx cy r) any-field) = #t"
     :meta "The CS pattern is closure-under-construction: the constructor lands inside the {sdf} type and every combinator keeps it there, so a pipeline of carves never falls out of the type. Falsified if some radius produced a value where sdf? returned #f — that would mean the constructor is partial and downstream booleans could not trust their inputs. book-of-code ch 05 (logic-gates-in-scheme) grounds the total-function-over-a-domain check; book-of-math ch-advanced-06 grounds the well-definedness of the level set.")
    (:eng "The engineering consequence is that a build pipeline can chain circle → subtract → union without re-validating types at each step; the type is an invariant the operator relies on." :math "sdf/circle : ℝ×ℝ×ℝ₊ → SDF is a total map onto the {sdf} type; the combinators are endomorphisms SDF×SDF → SDF." :cs "Closure under construction; the same shape as 'cons always returns a pair' — every builder in the family stays inside its type."))
  :row-5-emergence (

    (:base "sdf/circle alone makes one solid disc."
     :composition "(set-mode 'sd)\n;; a snowman: three stacked discs unioned into one field, painted once\n(let* ((base  (sdf/circle 160 180 34))\n       (belly (sdf/circle 160 130 26))\n       (head  (sdf/circle 160 90 18))\n       (body  (sdf-union (sdf-union base belly) head)))\n  (sdf-fill body 7))"
     :eng-composition "(set-mode 'sd)\n(define (stack-discs cx radii gap color)\n  ;; operator build: fold a list of radii into one union, then paint\n  (let loop ((rs radii) (y 200) (field #f))\n    (if (null? rs)\n        (sdf-fill field color)\n        (let ((disc (sdf/circle cx y (car rs))))\n          (loop (cdr rs) (- y gap) (if field (sdf-union field disc) disc))))))\n(stack-discs 160 (list 34 26 18) 50 7)"
     :math-composition "(set-mode 'sd)\n;; union = pointwise min of fields; fold min over a list of discs is one field\n(define discs (map (lambda (yr) (sdf/circle 160 (car yr) (cadr yr)))\n                   (list (list 180 34) (list 130 26) (list 90 18))))\n(sdf-fill (fold sdf-union (car discs) (cdr discs)) 7)"
     :cs-composition "(set-mode 'sd)\n;; map-then-reduce: map centers to fields, reduce with the union combinator\n(define (build-blob specs) (fold sdf-union (car specs) (cdr specs)))\n(sdf-fill (build-blob (map (lambda (s) (sdf/circle (car s) (cadr s) (caddr s)))\n                           (list (list 160 180 34) (list 160 130 26) (list 160 90 18))))\n          7)"
     :emergent "A snowman: three circle fields stacked and unioned so their overlaps melt into one smooth silhouette, painted in a single sweep — an object built from primitives it was never shown as a whole."
     :meta "This is the constructive-solid-geometry / implicit-surface family — simple distance fields combined by min/max arithmetic into recognizable objects. Familiar analogs are metaballs in graphics, boolean modeling in CAD, and blobby molecular surfaces in chemistry rendering. The composition works because union is pointwise min, so overlapping discs fuse instead of z-fighting, and the whole stack is still one {sdf} you paint once. Grounds in book-of-math ch-advanced-06 (analytic-geometry) as the union-of-level-sets shape.")
    (:eng "The audit lens is 'one paint call, not three' — a fold into a single field means the SRE cost is one canvas sweep regardless of how many discs, versus N sweeps if you painted each disc separately." :math "The union of circle sublevel-sets is the sublevel-set of their pointwise minimum; min is associative so the fold order does not matter." :cs "Map-then-reduce over a combinator; the same shape as summing a list, but the monoid is (SDF, sdf-union) instead of (number, +)."))
)

(verb
  :name "sdf/box" :std "Motoi"
  :library "sdf"
  :kind "meta"
  :signature "(sdf/box cx cy hw hh) -> {sdf}"
  :summary "Build a signed distance field for an axis-aligned rectangle centered at (cx, cy) with half-width hw and half-height hh."
  :explanation "Where the circle field measures radial distance, the box field measures rectangular distance — and the argument that trips people up is that hw and hh are HALF-extents, not full width and height. A box centered at (cx, cy) with hw=30 spans from cx−30 to cx+30, so it is 60 wide. Inside the rectangle the signed distance is negative (the distance to the nearest wall, made negative); outside it is the true Euclidean distance to the box, correctly rounding the corners. This half-extent convention is the natural one for signed distance because the field is symmetric about the center: hw and hh are exactly the distances from center to the two pairs of walls. The box is your source of corners, plates, bars, and window frames, and it pairs constantly with sdf/circle — box minus circle is a keyhole, box plus circle is a rounded tab."
  :examples (
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "novice" :code "(sdf? (sdf/box 100 100 30 20))" :note "A box field centered at (100,100) that reaches 30 pixels left/right and 20 up/down — a 60×40 rectangle described as an {sdf}, not yet painted.")
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "intermediate" :code "(set-mode 'sd)\n(sdf-fill (sdf/box 160 120 50 30) 9)" :note "Paint a solid 100×60 rectangle: the half-extents 50 and 30 double to the full width and height.")
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "expert" :code "(define (square-field cx cy half) (sdf/box cx cy half half))\n(map (lambda (h) (sdf? (square-field 100 100 h))) (list 10 20 40))" :note "Equal half-extents give a square; wrapping the constructor names the special case and confirms each is a valid field.")
  )
  :caveats ("hw and hh are HALF-extents: the full rectangle is 2·hw wide by 2·hh tall — the single most common mistake is passing full width." "Zero half-extents collapse the box to a line or point and it will not fill as a rectangle." "The box is axis-aligned; there is no rotation argument, so a tilted rectangle must be built from other primitives." "Like every field it paints nothing until handed to sdf-fill or sdf-fill-rect after (set-mode 'sd).")
  :drawbacks ("No rounded-corner radius parameter — the box has sharp corners; round them by unioning circle fields at the corners or intersecting with a large circle." "Axis-aligned only; a diagonal bar wants sdf/segment instead." "For a hollow frame you must subtract a smaller box, doubling the field cost.")
  :usecases ("Plates, bars, walls, and platforms in a game scene." "The outer body of a keyhole or washer before a circle is carved out." "A window frame built by subtracting a smaller box from a larger one." "A clipping region: intersect any field with a big box to crop it to a rectangle.")
  :related ("sdf/circle" "sdf/segment" "sdf-subtract" "sdf-intersect" "sdf-fill-rect")
  :learn (:concept "Half-extent parameterization: a centered box is described by its center and the half-distances to its walls, which is the symmetric, distance-natural way to specify a rectangle." :prerequisites ("sdf/circle" "sdf-fill") :progression "Build a box, paint it, then subtract a circle from its center to make a keyhole — the moment the half-extent convention becomes muscle memory.")
  :row-2-audit (

    (:problem "A level designer needs a platform 120 pixels wide and 24 tall centered under the player start. They compute the width they want, pass it straight into hw, and the platform comes out twice as wide as intended — the classic half-extent trap."
     :program "(set-mode 'sd)\n;; want 120 wide, 24 tall -> HALF those numbers\n(sdf-fill (sdf/box 160 200 60 12) 3)"
     :eng-program "(set-mode 'sd)\n(define (platform cx cy full-w full-h color)\n  ;; operator API in FULL dimensions; convert to half-extents internally\n  (sdf-fill (sdf/box cx cy (/ full-w 2) (/ full-h 2)) color))\n(platform 160 200 120 24 3)"
     :math-program "(define (box-extent cx cy hw hh)\n  ;; the walls are exactly center +/- half-extent\n  (list 'x-range (list (- cx hw) (+ cx hw))\n        'y-range (list (- cy hh) (+ cy hh))\n        'full-size (list (* 2 hw) (* 2 hh))))\n(box-extent 160 200 60 12)"
     :cs-program "(define (box-from-full cx cy w h)\n  (if (and (> w 0) (> h 0))\n      (sdf/box cx cy (/ w 2) (/ h 2))\n      (error 'nonpositive-box-size w h)))\n(sdf? (box-from-full 160 200 120 24))"
     :explanation "eng: expose a full-dimension API and halve internally so callers never touch the trap. math: the wall positions are center ± half-extent, making full size = 2·half. cs: a constructor that takes full width/height and guards positivity."
     :meta "sdf/circle rounds every edge and cannot make the crisp horizontal top a platform needs. sdf/segment makes a capsule with rounded caps, not a flat-topped slab. The raster rectangle verb paints immediately and cannot be cut into. sdf/box is the axis-aligned corner primitive — the only one here that gives a flat top you can still carve a trapdoor out of.")
    (:eng "The audit lens is 'full size = 2 × the argument' — the fastest smoke test after any box change is to eyeball width against 2·hw; if they disagree, someone passed full extent." :math "The box is {(x,y) : |x−cx| ≤ hw ∧ |y−cy| ≤ hh}; the half-extents are the L∞ radii along each axis." :cs "A closure over (cx, cy, hw, hh); the field evaluates a component-wise max of per-axis over-shoot, O(1) per point."))
  :row-3-dimension (
    (:setup "Describe a symmetric extent by its center and the half-distances to its limits, so the boundary is center ± half along each axis."
     :rungs (
       (:domain "sdf-box"        :code "(sdf? (sdf/box 100 100 30 20))")
       (:domain "tolerance-band" :code "(define (in-tolerance? target half-tol measured) (<= (abs (- measured target)) half-tol))\n(list (in-tolerance? 100 5 103) (in-tolerance? 100 5 108))")
       (:domain "interval"       :code "(define (interval center radius) (list (- center radius) (+ center radius)))\n(list (interval 100 30) (interval 100 20))"))
     :explanation "Rung 1: an SDF box is a center plus half-width and half-height. Rung 2: a manufacturing tolerance band is a target value plus a half-tolerance — a part passes if it is within ±half-tol. Rung 3: a numeric interval written as center ± radius is exactly the same [c−r, c+r]. All three share one shape — a symmetric range named by its midpoint and its half-width, not by its two endpoints."
     :meta "The transfer works because 'centered box' is a product of two 'center ± radius' intervals, one per axis. Every substrate that names a range by its midpoint and half-width — a tolerance spec, an error bar, a confidence half-interval — is using the half-extent idea. Grounds in book-of-math ch-advanced-06 (analytic-geometry) as the L∞-ball / interval-product shape.")
     (:eng "Every half-extent substrate shares one gotcha: 'is the parameter the half or the whole?' — the audit is to check that endpoint−center equals the stated half, on both sides." :math "A centered box is the Cartesian product of two intervals [cx−hw, cx+hw]×[cy−hh, cy+hh], i.e. an axis-aligned L∞ ball scaled per axis." :cs "Center-plus-radius encoding of a range; the same shape as representing an interval by midpoint and half-width rather than lo/hi."))
  :row-4-proof (
    (:claim "The box is symmetric about its center: reflecting a query point across (cx, cy) leaves its membership unchanged, and equal half-extents make a square."
     :program "(define (inside-box? cx cy hw hh x y)\n  (and (<= (abs (- x cx)) hw) (<= (abs (- y cy)) hh)))\n(define cx 100) (define cy 100) (define hw 30) (define hh 20)\n;; a point and its mirror across the center agree\n(display (eq? (inside-box? cx cy hw hh 120 110)\n              (inside-box? cx cy hw hh (- (* 2 cx) 120) (- (* 2 cy) 110)))) (newline)\n(display (eq? (inside-box? cx cy hw hh 140 100)\n              (inside-box? cx cy hw hh 60 100)))"
     :eng-program "(define (symmetry-audit cx cy hw hh probes)\n  ;; every probe must agree with its point-reflection through the center\n  (define (inside? x y) (and (<= (abs (- x cx)) hw) (<= (abs (- y cy)) hh)))\n  (fold (lambda (p acc)\n          (and acc (eq? (inside? (car p) (cadr p))\n                        (inside? (- (* 2 cx) (car p)) (- (* 2 cy) (cadr p))))))\n        #t probes))\n(symmetry-audit 100 100 30 20 (list (list 120 110) (list 140 100) (list 100 130)))"
     :math-program "(define (square? hw hh) (= hw hh))\n;; equal half-extents => a square field\n(list 'square (square? 25 25) 'oblong (square? 40 20))"
     :cs-program "(define (both-fields? hw hh)\n  (and (sdf? (sdf/box 100 100 hw hh))\n       (sdf? (sdf/box 100 100 hh hw))))  ;; swapping extents still a valid field\n(both-fields? 30 20)"
     :invariant "∀ cx cy hw hh. ∀ (x,y). inside(x,y) = inside(2cx−x, 2cy−y)  ∧  (hw = hh ⇒ the box is a square)"
     :meta "The CS pattern is a symmetry invariant — the field is unchanged under the reflection group generated by the two axes through the center. Falsified if a point were inside but its mirror image across the center were outside; that would mean the box is not centered where it claims, and the half-extent contract is broken. book-of-math ch-advanced-04 (groups-symmetry) grounds the reflection symmetry of a centered box; book-of-code ch 10 (state-machines) grounds invariance-under-a-transform.")
    (:eng "The engineering consequence is that a designer can place a box by its center and trust that nudging the center moves all four walls together; symmetry means one coordinate, not four, controls placement." :math "The box's membership function is invariant under the point reflection (x,y) ↦ (2cx−x, 2cy−y) and under each axis reflection; equal extents add the diagonal symmetry of a square." :cs "Invariance under a symmetry transform; the same shape as a palindrome check or a centrosymmetric data structure that reads the same from either end."))
  :row-5-emergence (

    (:base "sdf/box alone makes one flat-topped rectangle."
     :composition "(set-mode 'sd)\n;; a simple house: a square body with a triangle-ish cap made from a box union\n(let* ((body  (sdf/box 160 150 40 40))\n       (chim  (sdf/box 190 100 6 20))\n       (house (sdf-union body chim)))\n  (sdf-fill house 4))"
     :eng-composition "(set-mode 'sd)\n(define (framed-window cx cy hw hh thickness color)\n  ;; operator build: outer box minus inner box = a hollow frame\n  (let* ((outer (sdf/box cx cy hw hh))\n         (inner (sdf/box cx cy (- hw thickness) (- hh thickness)))\n         (frame (sdf-subtract outer inner)))\n    (sdf-fill frame color)))\n(framed-window 160 120 45 35 8 6)"
     :math-composition "(set-mode 'sd)\n;; subtraction = max(a, -b): a frame is outer-box minus inner-box\n(define outer (sdf/box 160 120 45 35))\n(define inner (sdf/box 160 120 37 27))\n(sdf-fill (sdf-subtract outer inner) 6)"
     :cs-composition "(set-mode 'sd)\n;; grid of tiles: map a coordinate list to box fields, reduce with union, paint once\n(define (grid nx ny step half base color)\n  (define coords\n    (apply append\n      (map (lambda (i)\n             (map (lambda (j) (sdf/box (+ base (* i step)) (+ base (* j step)) half half))\n                  (iota ny)))\n           (iota nx))))\n  (sdf-fill (fold sdf-union (car coords) (cdr coords)) color))\n(grid 3 3 40 14 60 5)"
     :emergent "A window frame (and a tiled grid): outer box minus inner box yields a hollow rectangular frame, and mapping boxes over a coordinate lattice yields a tiled wall — recognizable architecture assembled from one primitive by boolean arithmetic."
     :meta "This is the constructive-solid-geometry family again, now on the L∞ primitive — boxes combined by min (union) and max(a,−b) (subtraction) into frames, grids, and hollow shells. Familiar analogs are CAD sketch-and-extrude, CSS box-model nesting, and tilemap rendering. The composition works because subtraction is max(a,−b), which keeps everything an {sdf}, so a frame is not two shapes drawn over each other but a single carved field. Grounds in book-of-code ch 05 (logic-gates-in-scheme) as the nested-region / difference-of-sets shape."
     )
    (:eng "The audit lens is 'the frame is one field, so one paint call' — subtracting the inner box before painting means the SRE never draws-then-erases; the hole is baked into the field, not stamped over pixels." :math "outer ∖ inner = {p : p ∈ outer ∧ p ∉ inner}, realized as max(outer(p), −inner(p)) ≤ 0; a difference of two axis-aligned boxes." :cs "Difference of regions via max(a,−b); the same shape as set-difference on interval trees, computed pointwise on fields."))
)

(verb
  :name "sdf/segment" :std "Motoi"
  :library "sdf"
  :kind "meta"
  :signature "(sdf/segment x0 y0 x1 y1 thick) -> {sdf}"
  :summary "Build a signed distance field for a thick line segment (a capsule) from (x0,y0) to (x1,y1) with the given thickness."
  :explanation "The segment field is the odd one out among the constructors: it has no obvious 'center,' and it draws a STROKE rather than an area. Geometrically it is a capsule — the set of all points within distance thick of the line segment between the two endpoints — which is why its ends are rounded like a stadium, not squared off. The signed distance at any point is the distance to the nearest point on the segment, minus the half-thickness, so the field measures 'how far am I from the spine of this stroke.' This makes it the natural tool for anything drawn with lines: the arms and legs of a stick figure, the spokes of a wheel, a lightning bolt, a road, the strokes of a hand-drawn letter. Chain several segments end-to-end with sdf-union and you have a thick polyline; the rounded caps make the joints fuse smoothly instead of leaving gaps."
  :examples (
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "novice" :code "(sdf? (sdf/segment 20 20 100 100 4))" :note "A capsule field of thickness 4 running diagonally from (20,20) to (100,100) — an {sdf} value describing a rounded-cap stroke, painted by nothing yet.")
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "intermediate" :code "(set-mode 'sd)\n(sdf-fill (sdf/segment 40 200 280 60 6) 11)" :note "Paint a thick line across the canvas: every pixel within 6 of the segment lights up, and the two ends are rounded.")
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "expert" :code "(define pts (list (list 40 180) (list 120 60) (list 200 180) (list 280 60)))\n(define segs (map (lambda (a b) (sdf/segment (car a) (cadr a) (car b) (cadr b) 5))\n                  pts (cdr pts)))\n(map sdf? segs)" :note "Pair each point with the next (pts and (cdr pts)) to build the segments of a zig-zag polyline; every segment is an independent capsule field.")
  )
  :caveats ("thick is the FULL stroke width envelope; a very small thick makes a hairline that may miss pixels, a large one a fat bar." "The end caps are ROUNDED (it is a capsule); if you need square ends, intersect with a box or use sdf/box." "A zero-length segment (both endpoints equal) degenerates to a disc of radius thick — sometimes useful, sometimes a bug." "It draws the stroke, not a filled area between strokes; a filled polygon needs unions and intersections, not a single segment.")
  :drawbacks ("No square or beveled caps — the rounded cap is baked in." "A closed outline (like a triangle) needs three segments unioned; the segment alone cannot close a loop." "Thick strokes overlapping at a sharp joint bulge at the corner because both capsules cover it; that is the price of smooth fusion.")
  :usecases ("Limbs and spine of a stick figure, unioned with a circle head." "Spokes of a wheel or rays of a sun, one segment per spoke." "A polyline path — a road, a river, a wire — built from end-to-end segments." "The strokes of a letter or a signature, each pen-down-to-pen-up a segment.")
  :related ("sdf/circle" "sdf/box" "sdf-union" "sdf-fill" "sdf-fill-rect")
  :learn (:concept "A capsule as the thickened level set of a line segment: points within a fixed distance of a 1-D spine, giving a stroke with rounded caps." :prerequisites ("sdf/circle" "sdf-union") :progression "Draw one segment, then union two segments sharing an endpoint to see the rounded caps fuse into a smooth elbow — the basis of every polyline.")
  :row-2-audit (

    (:problem "A cart author is drawing a stick figure and needs the arms, legs, and torso to be strokes of even thickness that join cleanly at the shoulders and hips, without ugly gaps or square notches where two limbs meet."
     :program "(set-mode 'sd)\n(let ((torso (sdf/segment 100 70 100 120 5))\n      (arm-l (sdf/segment 100 85 72 110 5))\n      (arm-r (sdf/segment 100 85 128 110 5))\n      (leg-l (sdf/segment 100 120 78 165 5))\n      (leg-r (sdf/segment 100 120 122 165 5)))\n  (sdf-fill (sdf-union (sdf-union (sdf-union (sdf-union torso arm-l) arm-r) leg-l) leg-r) 7))"
     :eng-program "(set-mode 'sd)\n(define (stroke-path pts thick)\n  ;; operator build: fold a point list into a unioned polyline field\n  (let ((segs (map (lambda (a b) (sdf/segment (car a) (cadr a) (car b) (cadr b) thick))\n                   pts (cdr pts))))\n    (fold sdf-union (car segs) (cdr segs))))\n(sdf-fill (stroke-path (list (list 100 70) (list 100 120) (list 78 165)) 5) 7)"
     :math-program "(define (seg-dist x0 y0 x1 y1 px py)\n  ;; distance from point to segment via clamped projection parameter t\n  (let* ((dx (- x1 x0)) (dy (- y1 y0))\n         (len2 (+ (* dx dx) (* dy dy)))\n         (t (if (= len2 0) 0 (max 0 (min 1 (/ (+ (* (- px x0) dx) (* (- py y0) dy)) len2)))))\n         (cx (+ x0 (* t dx))) (cy (+ y0 (* t dy))))\n    (sqrt (+ (* (- px cx) (- px cx)) (* (- py cy) (- py cy))))))\n(list (seg-dist 0 0 10 0 5 3) (seg-dist 0 0 10 0 -4 0))"
     :cs-program "(define (safe-segment x0 y0 x1 y1 thick)\n  (if (and (> thick 0) (not (and (= x0 x1) (= y0 y1))))\n      (sdf/segment x0 y0 x1 y1 thick)\n      (error 'degenerate-segment x0 y0 x1 y1 thick)))\n(sdf? (safe-segment 100 70 100 120 5))"
     :explanation "eng: fold a point list into one unioned polyline so joints fuse. math: the field is distance-to-segment via clamped projection minus half-thickness. cs: a guard rejecting zero-length or zero-thickness segments before building."
     :meta "sdf/box makes flat bars with square ends that leave notches at diagonal joints. sdf/circle makes discs, not directional strokes. The raster line verb paints hairline pixels immediately and cannot be unioned into a fused figure. sdf/segment is the capsule primitive whose rounded caps are exactly what makes unioned limbs join without gaps.")
    (:eng "The audit lens is 'do the joints fuse?' — the smoke test is to union two segments at a shared endpoint and confirm no gap; the rounded caps guarantee it, a square-capped stroke would not." :math "The capsule is {p : dist(p, segment) ≤ thick/2}; distance-to-segment is |p − (a + clamp(t)(b−a))| with t the projection parameter." :cs "A closure over (a, b, thick); each evaluation clamps a projection parameter to [0,1] then measures Euclidean distance, O(1) per point."))
  :row-3-dimension (
    (:setup "Measure distance to a one-dimensional spine, then declare everything within a fixed band of it to be 'on the stroke.'"
     :rungs (
       (:domain "sdf-segment"    :code "(sdf? (sdf/segment 20 20 100 100 4))")
       (:domain "road-corridor"  :code "(define (within-corridor? road-x0 road-y0 road-x1 road-y1 half-width px py)\n  (let* ((dx (- road-x1 road-x0)) (dy (- road-y1 road-y0))\n         (len2 (+ (* dx dx) (* dy dy)))\n         (t (max 0 (min 1 (/ (+ (* (- px road-x0) dx) (* (- py road-y0) dy)) len2))))\n         (cx (+ road-x0 (* t dx))) (cy (+ road-y0 (* t dy))))\n    (<= (sqrt (+ (* (- px cx) (- px cx)) (* (- py cy) (- py cy)))) half-width)))\n(list (within-corridor? 0 0 100 0 5 50 3) (within-corridor? 0 0 100 0 5 50 20))")
       (:domain "tube-neighborhood" :code "(define (in-tube? spine-lo spine-hi radius t) (and (>= t spine-lo) (<= t spine-hi) (<= radius radius)))\n(list (in-tube? 0 100 4 40) (in-tube? 0 100 4 140))"))
     :explanation "Rung 1: an SDF segment is a band of fixed thickness around a line spine. Rung 2: a road corridor is everything within a half-width of the road's centerline — the same clamped-projection distance test. Rung 3: a tubular neighborhood in geometry is all points within radius ε of a curve. All three share one shape — take a 1-D spine, thicken it by a fixed radius, and ask whether a point falls inside that thickened region."
     :meta "The transfer works because a capsule is a 'tubular neighborhood' of a segment — the ε-thickening of a curve. Any substrate that buffers a path by a width — a road corridor, a GIS buffer around a river, a safety margin around a wire — is computing this same distance-to-spine field. Grounds in book-of-math ch-advanced-06 (analytic-geometry) as the offset-curve / tubular-neighborhood shape."
     )
     (:eng "Every spine-thickening substrate shares one probe: 'a point on the centerline reads distance zero, a point exactly at the half-width reads the boundary' — check those two and the band is calibrated." :math "The tubular neighborhood N_ε(C) = {p : dist(p, C) ≤ ε} of a curve C; for a segment, dist is the clamped-projection distance." :cs "Buffer-a-path-by-a-radius; the same shape as a GIS line buffer or a Minkowski sum of a segment with a disc of radius thick/2."))
  :row-4-proof (
    (:claim "The segment is endpoint-symmetric: swapping (x0,y0) with (x1,y1) describes the same capsule, and both orderings yield an {sdf}."
     :program "(define (seg-dist x0 y0 x1 y1 px py)\n  (let* ((dx (- x1 x0)) (dy (- y1 y0))\n         (len2 (+ (* dx dx) (* dy dy)))\n         (t (if (= len2 0) 0 (max 0 (min 1 (/ (+ (* (- px x0) dx) (* (- py y0) dy)) len2)))))\n         (cx (+ x0 (* t dx))) (cy (+ y0 (* t dy))))\n    (sqrt (+ (* (- px cx) (- px cx)) (* (- py cy) (- py cy))))))\n;; forward and reversed endpoints give the same distance to any probe point\n(display (< (abs (- (seg-dist 20 20 100 100 60 40)\n                    (seg-dist 100 100 20 20 60 40))) 0.0001)) (newline)\n(display (< (abs (- (seg-dist 20 20 100 100 10 90)\n                    (seg-dist 100 100 20 20 10 90))) 0.0001))"
     :eng-program "(define (reversal-audit x0 y0 x1 y1 thick)\n  ;; both endpoint orders must build a valid field\n  (and (sdf? (sdf/segment x0 y0 x1 y1 thick))\n       (sdf? (sdf/segment x1 y1 x0 y0 thick))))\n(reversal-audit 20 20 100 100 4)"
     :math-program "(define (midpoint x0 y0 x1 y1) (list (/ (+ x0 x1) 2) (/ (+ y0 y1) 2)))\n;; the capsule is symmetric about the midpoint of its two endpoints\n(midpoint 20 20 100 100)"
     :cs-program "(define (order-independent-field? x0 y0 x1 y1 thick)\n  (and (sdf? (sdf/segment x0 y0 x1 y1 thick))\n       (sdf? (sdf/segment x1 y1 x0 y0 thick))))\n(order-independent-field? 40 200 280 60 6)"
     :invariant "∀ a b thick > 0. ∀ p. dist(p, capsule(a,b,thick)) = dist(p, capsule(b,a,thick))  (the capsule does not depend on which endpoint is called first)"
     :meta "The CS pattern is argument-order invariance — the segment is an unordered pair of endpoints, so the field is symmetric under swapping them. Falsified if reversing the endpoints changed which pixels the capsule covered; that would mean the field secretly depends on a direction the geometry does not have. book-of-math ch-advanced-04 (groups-symmetry) grounds the reflection symmetry of a segment about its midpoint; book-of-code ch 10 (state-machines) grounds order-independence of a commutative input."
     )
    (:eng "The engineering consequence is that a path author can list the endpoints of a stroke in either order and get the same picture; the API has no hidden direction to get wrong." :math "A segment is the unordered set {a, b} thickened; distance-to-segment is symmetric in a and b because the underlying point set is." :cs "Commutativity of an unordered-pair argument; the same shape as an undirected edge that reads the same as (u,v) or (v,u)."))
  :row-5-emergence (

    (:base "sdf/segment alone makes one rounded-cap stroke."
     :composition "(set-mode 'sd)\n;; a five-pointed star OUTLINE from five unioned segments\n(let* ((cx 160) (cy 120) (r 70)\n       (pt (lambda (k) (let ((a (- (* k 1.2566) 1.5708)))\n                          (list (+ cx (* r (cos a))) (+ cy (* r (sin a)))))))\n       (p0 (pt 0)) (p2 (pt 2)) (p4 (pt 4)) (p1 (pt 1)) (p3 (pt 3))\n       (seg (lambda (a b) (sdf/segment (car a) (cadr a) (car b) (cadr b) 3))))\n  (sdf-fill (fold sdf-union (seg p0 p2) (list (seg p2 p4) (seg p4 p1) (seg p1 p3) (seg p3 p0))) 10))"
     :eng-composition "(set-mode 'sd)\n(define (sun cx cy r rays thick color)\n  ;; operator build: a hub disc plus N radial spokes, all one field\n  (let* ((hub (sdf/circle cx cy 16))\n         (spokes (map (lambda (k)\n                        (let ((a (* k (/ 6.2832 rays))))\n                          (sdf/segment cx cy (+ cx (* r (cos a))) (+ cy (* r (sin a))) thick)))\n                      (iota rays))))\n    (sdf-fill (fold sdf-union hub spokes) color)))\n(sun 160 120 70 12 3 10)"
     :math-composition "(set-mode 'sd)\n;; polyline = union (pointwise min) of consecutive-endpoint segments\n(define pts (list (list 40 180) (list 110 70) (list 180 180) (list 250 70) (list 290 160)))\n(define segs (map (lambda (a b) (sdf/segment (car a) (cadr a) (car b) (cadr b) 4)) pts (cdr pts)))\n(sdf-fill (fold sdf-union (car segs) (cdr segs)) 11)"
     :cs-composition "(set-mode 'sd)\n;; map adjacency pairs to capsules, reduce with union — a graph drawn as strokes\n(define (draw-edges nodes edges thick color)\n  (define (node i) (list-ref nodes i))\n  (define fields (map (lambda (e)\n                        (let ((a (node (car e))) (b (node (cadr e))))\n                          (sdf/segment (car a) (cadr a) (car b) (cadr b) thick)))\n                      edges))\n  (sdf-fill (fold sdf-union (car fields) (cdr fields)) color))\n(draw-edges (list (list 60 60) (list 260 60) (list 160 190))\n            (list (list 0 1) (list 1 2) (list 2 0)) 4 10)"
     :emergent "A star, a sun, a polyline, and a drawn graph: unioning capsules that share endpoints fuses them into a single connected stroke-figure — line-art assembled from one primitive by repeated union."
     :meta "This is the constructive-solid-geometry family on the capsule primitive — strokes fused by union (pointwise min) into connected line-art. Familiar analogs are SVG polyline rendering, stroked pen paths in vector graphics, and edge-drawing in graph layout. The composition works because rounded caps mean any two segments sharing an endpoint overlap in a disc, so their union has no seam — the whole figure is one {sdf}, painted once. Grounds in book-of-math ch-advanced-06 (analytic-geometry) as the union-of-tubular-neighborhoods shape."
     )
    (:eng "The audit lens is 'connected strokes = one field = one paint' — the SRE cost of a 100-edge graph is one canvas sweep, not 100 line-draws, and the rounded caps guarantee no gaps at the nodes." :math "A polyline's capsule union is the ε-neighborhood of the whole piecewise-linear curve; min over segment fields is the neighborhood of the union of segments." :cs "Map edges to capsules, reduce with union; the same map-reduce as drawing a graph, but the reducer is (SDF, sdf-union) so the strokes fuse."))
)

(verb
  :name "sdf-union" :std "Motoi"
  :library "sdf"
  :kind "meta"
  :signature "(sdf-union a b) -> {sdf}"
  :summary "Combine two signed distance fields by taking the pointwise minimum, producing a field for the union of the two shapes."
  :explanation "Here is where the SDF idea earns its keep: booleans on shapes become arithmetic on distances. To ask whether a point is inside shape A OR shape B, you do not test two shapes — you take the SMALLER of their two signed distances. If a point is inside A its A-distance is negative; the minimum of a negative number and anything is still negative, so the point is inside the union. sdf-union computes min(f_a(x,y), f_b(x,y)) at every point, and the resulting field is the union of the two shapes: everything covered by either one. Because it is min, overlapping shapes fuse into a single smooth silhouette rather than z-fighting or leaving seams. This is the OR of constructive solid geometry, and it is the workhorse you use to grow a snowman from discs, a figure from limbs, a blob from overlapping circles."
  :examples (
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "novice" :code "(sdf? (sdf-union (sdf/circle 100 100 30) (sdf/box 130 100 20 20)))" :note "Union a circle and a box into one field; the result is still an {sdf} — a single shape covering everything in either the disc or the rectangle.")
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "intermediate" :code "(set-mode 'sd)\n(sdf-fill (sdf-union (sdf/circle 130 120 34) (sdf/circle 190 120 34)) 12)" :note "Two overlapping circles unioned and painted read as one peanut-shaped blob because min fuses their overlap seamlessly.")
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "expert" :code "(define discs (map (lambda (i) (sdf/circle (+ 60 (* i 30)) 120 20)) (iota 5)))\n(sdf? (fold sdf-union (car discs) (cdr discs)))" :note "Fold sdf-union over a list of circle fields (element-first fold) to combine any number of shapes into one union field.")
  )
  :caveats ("Union is min, so the result is inside if EITHER shape is inside; do not expect it to mask or clip — that is intersection or subtraction." "Fusing overlapping shapes is a feature, not a bug: two touching discs become one blob with no visible seam." "Away from the boundary the min of two true distance fields is only an approximate distance field (it can under-estimate near concave joints), but the ≤ 0 fill region is exact." "It combines exactly two fields; for a list, fold sdf-union over them.")
  :drawbacks ("Deeply nested unions build a chain of min operations evaluated at every sampled pixel, so cost grows with the number of shapes." "The fused silhouette hides the individual shapes — you cannot color the parts differently in one union." "min breaks the exact-distance property at concave creases, which matters only if you later rely on the distance magnitude, not just its sign.")
  :usecases ("Growing a compound body — snowman, cloud, blob — from many overlapping primitives." "Assembling a figure from limb segments and a head circle into one silhouette." "Merging separate parts of a scene into a single field for one paint call." "Building the outer solid before carving detail out of it with sdf-subtract.")
  :related ("sdf-intersect" "sdf-subtract" "sdf/circle" "sdf/box" "sdf-fill")
  :learn (:concept "Union as pointwise minimum: a point is in A∪B iff its distance to the nearer shape is ≤ 0, and min picks the nearer distance." :prerequisites ("sdf/circle" "sdf-fill") :progression "Union two overlapping circles and watch them fuse; then contrast with sdf-intersect (max) on the same two circles to feel min-vs-max as OR-vs-AND.")
  :row-2-audit (

    (:problem "A cart author is building a cloud from several overlapping puffs. Painting each circle separately leaves visible outlines where the discs cross, and the cloud looks like a pile of coins instead of one soft mass. They want the puffs to melt into a single shape."
     :program "(set-mode 'sd)\n(sdf-fill (sdf-union (sdf-union (sdf/circle 120 120 26) (sdf/circle 160 110 32))\n                     (sdf/circle 200 120 26)) 7)"
     :eng-program "(set-mode 'sd)\n(define (cloud puffs color)\n  ;; operator build: fold a list of (cx cy r) puffs into one unioned field\n  (let ((fields (map (lambda (p) (sdf/circle (car p) (cadr p) (caddr p))) puffs)))\n    (sdf-fill (fold sdf-union (car fields) (cdr fields)) color)))\n(cloud (list (list 120 120 26) (list 160 110 32) (list 200 120 26)) 7)"
     :math-program "(define (union-dist da db) (min da db))\n;; a point inside EITHER shape: its union distance is negative\n(list 'inside-a (union-dist -3 5) 'inside-b (union-dist 4 -2) 'inside-both (union-dist -1 -6) 'outside (union-dist 3 8))"
     :cs-program "(define (union-all fields)\n  (if (null? fields) (error 'empty-union)\n      (fold sdf-union (car fields) (cdr fields))))\n(sdf? (union-all (map (lambda (p) (sdf/circle (car p) (cadr p) 24))\n                      (list (list 120 120) (list 160 110) (list 200 120)))))"
     :explanation "eng: fold puffs into one field so the cloud is a single silhouette. math: min of the two distances is negative whenever either is, which is exactly OR. cs: a union-all that folds a list and guards the empty case."
     :meta "sdf-intersect (max) would keep only the overlap of the puffs — a tiny lens, not a cloud. sdf-subtract would carve one puff out of another, leaving a bite. Painting each circle with sdf-fill separately draws seams because each is a fresh threshold, not a fused field. sdf-union is the OR primitive: min fuses overlaps into one seamless mass.")
    (:eng "The audit lens is 'no seams' — the smoke test is to overlap two shapes and confirm the union paints as one silhouette with no internal outline; a seam means you painted separately instead of unioning." :math "The union's field is min(f_a, f_b); the ≤ 0 region is {p : f_a(p) ≤ 0 ∨ f_b(p) ≤ 0} = A ∪ B." :cs "Pointwise min of two field closures; O(1) per point per union, O(k) for a k-way fold."))
  :row-3-dimension (
    (:setup "Combine two membership scores so that belonging to EITHER wins — take the value that most strongly says 'inside.'"
     :rungs (
       (:domain "sdf-union"   :code "(sdf? (sdf-union (sdf/circle 100 100 30) (sdf/box 130 100 20 20)))")
       (:domain "coverage-map" :code "(define (covered? tower-a-dist tower-b-dist range) (<= (min tower-a-dist tower-b-dist) range))\n(list (covered? 8 3 5) (covered? 8 9 5))")
       (:domain "fuzzy-or"    :code "(define (fuzzy-or a b) (max a b))  ;; membership in [0,1]; OR = max\n(list (fuzzy-or 0.2 0.9) (fuzzy-or 0.1 0.0))"))
     :explanation "Rung 1: an SDF union is the pointwise min of two distance fields — inside if either is. Rung 2: a cell-coverage map says a phone is served if it is within range of the NEARER tower, i.e. the min distance is small enough. Rung 3: fuzzy logic's OR is the max of two membership values in [0,1]. All three share one shape — combining two 'how much do I belong' scores with a single extremum so that belonging to either input carries through."
     :meta "The transfer works because union is the OR of set theory realized as an extremum on a real-valued membership signal. Whether the signal is a signed distance (min = OR because inside is negative) or a fuzzy membership (max = OR because inside is high), the operation is 'take the value that most says inside.' This is exactly the De Morgan-dual of intersection. Grounds in book-of-math ch-advanced-12 (linear-algebra-deep is wrong here) — grounds in book-of-code ch 05 (logic-gates-in-scheme) as the OR-gate-on-a-continuous-signal shape."
     )
     (:eng "Every union substrate shares one probe: 'if a point is inside exactly one input, is it inside the combination?' — union must say yes, and the extremum (min for distances, max for memberships) is what guarantees it." :math "Union corresponds to the join in the lattice of shapes; on signed distances the join is min, on [0,1] memberships it is max — dual encodings of the same Boolean OR." :cs "An OR realized as an extremum over a real signal; the same shape as a fuzzy-OR, a nearest-of-many coverage test, and a soft-max-style 'strongest wins' combiner."))
  :row-4-proof (
    (:claim "sdf-union is commutative: (sdf-union a b) and (sdf-union b a) describe the same shape, because min is commutative."
     :program "(define (union-dist da db) (min da db))\n;; min is commutative, so union does not depend on argument order\n(display (= (union-dist -3 5) (union-dist 5 -3))) (newline)\n(display (= (union-dist 4 -2) (union-dist -2 4))) (newline)\n(define a (sdf/circle 100 100 30))\n(define b (sdf/box 130 100 20 20))\n(display (and (sdf? (sdf-union a b)) (sdf? (sdf-union b a))))"
     :eng-program "(define (commutes-and-associates? a b c)\n  ;; both orderings and both groupings must stay valid fields\n  (and (sdf? (sdf-union a b)) (sdf? (sdf-union b a))\n       (sdf? (sdf-union (sdf-union a b) c))\n       (sdf? (sdf-union a (sdf-union b c)))))\n(commutes-and-associates? (sdf/circle 80 100 15) (sdf/circle 100 100 15) (sdf/circle 120 100 15))"
     :math-program "(define (min-commutes? x y) (= (min x y) (min y x)))\n(define (min-associates? x y z) (= (min (min x y) z) (min x (min y z))))\n(list 'commutes (min-commutes? -3 5) 'associates (min-associates? -3 5 2))"
     :cs-program "(define (fold-order-independent? fields)\n  ;; folding a union in forward vs reversed order both yield a field\n  (and (sdf? (fold sdf-union (car fields) (cdr fields)))\n       (sdf? (fold sdf-union (car (reverse fields)) (cdr (reverse fields))))))\n(fold-order-independent? (map (lambda (i) (sdf/circle (* i 30) 100 12)) (iota 4 1)))"
     :invariant "∀ a b. shape(sdf-union a b) = shape(sdf-union b a)  ∧  shape(sdf-union (sdf-union a b) c) = shape(sdf-union a (sdf-union b c))   (union is commutative and associative because min is)"
     :meta "The CS pattern is that union inherits its algebra from min: min is a commutative, associative, idempotent operation, so union is a commutative monoid (with the empty/everywhere-outside field as identity). Falsified if unioning a with b covered different pixels than unioning b with a — that would mean min is not commutative, which is impossible for a total order. book-of-math ch-advanced-04 (groups-symmetry) grounds the commutative-monoid structure; book-of-code ch 11 (the-alu) grounds min as a primitive commutative operation."
     )
    (:eng "The engineering consequence is that a fold over many shapes can be evaluated in any order — parallelized or reordered — and produce the identical union; the operator never has to worry about the order shapes were added." :math "(SDF, sdf-union) is a commutative monoid because (ℝ, min) is; commutativity and associativity of min lift pointwise to fields." :cs "Commutative-associative reducer; the same algebra as summing numbers or unioning sets — fold order is irrelevant to the result."))
  :row-5-emergence (

    (:base "sdf-union alone fuses two fields into one."
     :composition "(set-mode 'sd)\n;; a dumbbell: two heavy end-discs joined by a thick bar, all unioned\n(let* ((left  (sdf/circle 90 120 26))\n       (right (sdf/circle 230 120 26))\n       (bar   (sdf/box 160 120 55 8))\n       (weight (sdf-union (sdf-union left right) bar)))\n  (sdf-fill weight 3))"
     :eng-composition "(set-mode 'sd)\n(define (chain-of-beads cx0 cy dx n r color)\n  ;; operator build: n discs in a row folded into one union field\n  (let ((beads (map (lambda (i) (sdf/circle (+ cx0 (* i dx)) cy r)) (iota n))))\n    (sdf-fill (fold sdf-union (car beads) (cdr beads)) color)))\n(chain-of-beads 60 120 40 6 16 11)"
     :math-composition "(set-mode 'sd)\n;; a metaball blob: many overlapping discs, union = pointwise min, one silhouette\n(define blobs (map (lambda (p) (sdf/circle (car p) (cadr p) (caddr p)))\n                   (list (list 130 120 28) (list 160 100 30) (list 190 120 28) (list 160 140 26))))\n(sdf-fill (fold sdf-union (car blobs) (cdr blobs)) 12)"
     :cs-composition "(set-mode 'sd)\n;; map a parametric ring of circles, reduce with union — a flower of petals\n(define (petal-ring cx cy r petal n color)\n  (let ((petals (map (lambda (k)\n                       (let ((a (* k (/ 6.2832 n))))\n                         (sdf/circle (+ cx (* r (cos a))) (+ cy (* r (sin a))) petal)))\n                     (iota n))))\n    (sdf-fill (fold sdf-union (sdf/circle cx cy 14) petals) color)))\n(petal-ring 160 120 46 18 8 12)"
     :emergent "A dumbbell, a bead chain, a metaball blob, a flower: repeated union of primitives grows a single connected object whose parts have melted into one silhouette — compound bodies no primitive could make alone."
     :meta "This is the constructive-solid-geometry / metaball family — union (pointwise min) as the OR that grows compound solids from overlapping simples. Familiar analogs are metaball surfaces in graphics, blob merging in fluid rendering, and set-union in region algebras. The composition works because union is a commutative monoid (Row 4), so folding any number of shapes in any order gives one coherent silhouette, painted once. Grounds in book-of-code ch 05 (logic-gates-in-scheme) as the many-input-OR / reduce-with-OR shape."
     )
    (:eng "The audit lens is 'k shapes, one paint' — folding a union means the SRE cost is one canvas sweep over a k-deep min chain, versus k separate paints that also leave seams; fewer sweeps and no seams." :math "A k-way union is the reduction of min over k fields, the lattice join of k shapes; the fused silhouette is the sublevel set of the pointwise minimum." :cs "Reduce-with-a-commutative-monoid; the same shape as summing a list or OR-ing a bitset, with (SDF, sdf-union) as the monoid."))
)

(verb
  :name "sdf-intersect" :std "Motoi"
  :library "sdf"
  :kind "meta"
  :signature "(sdf-intersect a b) -> {sdf}"
  :summary "Combine two signed distance fields by taking the pointwise maximum, producing a field for the intersection of the two shapes."
  :explanation "If union is OR, intersection is AND — and it flips min to max. A point is inside A AND B only when it is inside both, so its distance in the combined field must report 'outside' unless BOTH inputs report 'inside.' Taking the maximum of the two signed distances does exactly this: a point is inside the intersection (max ≤ 0) only when neither distance is positive, i.e. both are ≤ 0. sdf-intersect computes max(f_a, f_b) at every point. The result keeps only the overlap of the two shapes and throws away everything else. Two overlapping circles intersect to a lens; a circle intersected with a box crops the circle to the rectangle. Intersection is how you clip, mask, and trim — it is the AND that carves a shape down to the region two shapes have in common."
  :examples (
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "novice" :code "(sdf? (sdf-intersect (sdf/circle 140 120 40) (sdf/circle 180 120 40)))" :note "Intersect two overlapping circles; the result {sdf} covers only the lens-shaped region both circles contain.")
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "intermediate" :code "(set-mode 'sd)\n(sdf-fill (sdf-intersect (sdf/circle 160 120 60) (sdf/box 160 120 40 25)) 12)" :note "Crop a circle to a rectangle: the painted shape is the circle clipped to the box, a rounded-cornered rectangle where the two overlap.")
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "expert" :code "(define (lens r sep) (sdf-intersect (sdf/circle (- 160 sep) 120 r) (sdf/circle (+ 160 sep) 120 r)))\n(map (lambda (s) (sdf? (lens 40 s))) (list 10 20 30))" :note "Parameterize a lens by radius and separation; as the two circles pull apart the overlap shrinks, but every result is a valid {sdf}.")
  )
  :caveats ("Intersection is max, so the result is inside ONLY where both shapes are inside; if the shapes do not overlap, the intersection is empty and paints nothing." "Use it to clip and mask, not to combine — for combining reach for sdf-union." "Like min for union, max only approximates true distance away from the boundary near convex creases, but the ≤ 0 fill region is exact." "It intersects exactly two fields; for a list, fold sdf-intersect over them — but remember every added shape can only shrink the result.")
  :drawbacks ("Non-overlapping inputs give an empty shape with no warning — a blank canvas, which can look like a bug." "Each additional intersected shape only removes area, so a long chain quickly whittles down to nothing." "The result hides which input contributed which edge, so you cannot re-color the parts separately.")
  :usecases ("Clipping a shape to a rectangular or circular window." "Making a lens or vesica from two overlapping circles." "Masking a complex field down to a region of interest before painting." "Enforcing a boundary: intersect any built-up shape with a frame box to guarantee it stays on-canvas.")
  :related ("sdf-union" "sdf-subtract" "sdf/circle" "sdf/box" "sdf-fill-rect")
  :learn (:concept "Intersection as pointwise maximum: a point is in A∩B iff BOTH distances are ≤ 0, and max is ≤ 0 exactly when neither input is positive." :prerequisites ("sdf-union" "sdf/circle") :progression "Intersect the same two overlapping circles you unioned earlier and compare: union (min) gives a peanut, intersection (max) gives the lens between them — OR versus AND made visible.")
  :row-2-audit (

    (:problem "A cart author has a decorative circular emblem but the scene only has a rectangular banner slot for it. They need the emblem trimmed to the banner's rectangle — anything outside the slot must vanish — without redrawing the emblem from scratch."
     :program "(set-mode 'sd)\n(sdf-fill (sdf-intersect (sdf/circle 160 120 55) (sdf/box 160 120 45 22)) 6)"
     :eng-program "(set-mode 'sd)\n(define (clip-to-slot field slot-cx slot-cy slot-hw slot-hh color)\n  ;; operator build: AND the emblem with the banner rectangle\n  (sdf-fill (sdf-intersect field (sdf/box slot-cx slot-cy slot-hw slot-hh)) color))\n(clip-to-slot (sdf/circle 160 120 55) 160 120 45 22 6)"
     :math-program "(define (intersect-dist da db) (max da db))\n;; inside ONLY when both distances are non-positive\n(list 'both-in (intersect-dist -3 -1) 'only-a (intersect-dist -3 4) 'only-b (intersect-dist 5 -2) 'neither (intersect-dist 3 8))"
     :cs-program "(define (clip field window)\n  (if (and (sdf? field) (sdf? window))\n      (sdf-intersect field window)\n      (error 'clip-needs-two-fields)))\n(sdf? (clip (sdf/circle 160 120 55) (sdf/box 160 120 45 22)))"
     :explanation "eng: AND the emblem with the banner box so out-of-slot pixels vanish. math: max of the two distances is ≤ 0 only when both are, which is exactly AND. cs: a clip helper that type-checks both operands are fields."
     :meta "sdf-union (min) would ADD the banner rectangle to the emblem, not trim it. sdf-subtract would cut the banner OUT of the emblem, leaving a bite. Painting the emblem then drawing a rectangle over it cannot remove pixels already outside the slot. sdf-intersect is the AND primitive: max keeps only the overlap, so the emblem is cropped to the slot in one field.")
    (:eng "The audit lens is 'only the overlap survives' — the smoke test is to intersect two barely-overlapping shapes and confirm you get the small common region, not the sum; a large result means you unioned by mistake." :math "The intersection's field is max(f_a, f_b); the ≤ 0 region is {p : f_a(p) ≤ 0 ∧ f_b(p) ≤ 0} = A ∩ B." :cs "Pointwise max of two field closures; O(1) per point, and each added intersect can only shrink the ≤ 0 set."))
  :row-3-dimension (
    (:setup "Combine two membership scores so that only belonging to BOTH survives — take the value that most strongly says 'outside.'"
     :rungs (
       (:domain "sdf-intersect" :code "(sdf? (sdf-intersect (sdf/circle 140 120 40) (sdf/circle 180 120 40)))")
       (:domain "access-control" :code "(define (granted? has-role? in-hours?) (and has-role? in-hours?))\n(list (granted? #t #t) (granted? #t #f))")
       (:domain "fuzzy-and"     :code "(define (fuzzy-and a b) (min a b))  ;; membership in [0,1]; AND = min\n(list (fuzzy-and 0.8 0.3) (fuzzy-and 0.9 1.0))"))
     :explanation "Rung 1: an SDF intersection is the pointwise max of two distance fields — inside only if both are. Rung 2: an access-control gate grants entry only when the user has the role AND is within allowed hours; either failing denies. Rung 3: fuzzy logic's AND is the min of two membership values in [0,1]. All three share one shape — combining two 'how much do I belong' scores with a single extremum so that failing EITHER input fails the combination."
     :meta "The transfer works because intersection is the AND of set theory realized as an extremum on a real-valued membership signal — and it is the exact dual of union. On signed distances, inside is negative so AND takes max (the more-positive, more-outside value wins unless both are inside); on fuzzy memberships, inside is high so AND takes min. Same Boolean AND, dual encodings. Grounds in book-of-code ch 05 (logic-gates-in-scheme) as the AND-gate-on-a-continuous-signal shape."
     )
     (:eng "Every intersection substrate shares one probe: 'if a point fails exactly one input, is it excluded from the combination?' — intersection must say yes, and the extremum (max for distances, min for memberships) is what enforces it." :math "Intersection is the meet in the lattice of shapes; on signed distances the meet is max, on [0,1] memberships it is min — dual encodings of the same Boolean AND, related to union by De Morgan." :cs "An AND realized as an extremum over a real signal; the same shape as a fuzzy-AND, an all-conditions-must-pass gate, and a min-pooling 'weakest link' combiner."))
  :row-4-proof (
    (:claim "sdf-intersect is commutative, and intersecting a shape with a large enclosing box is the identity on the region the box contains."
     :program "(define (intersect-dist da db) (max da db))\n;; max is commutative, so intersection ignores argument order\n(display (= (intersect-dist -3 -1) (intersect-dist -1 -3))) (newline)\n(display (= (intersect-dist 5 -2) (intersect-dist -2 5))) (newline)\n;; intersecting with a huge box leaves an inside point inside (identity there)\n(define big-box-dist -100)  ;; a point deep inside a large enclosing box\n(define shape-dist -4)      ;; the same point inside the shape\n(display (= (intersect-dist shape-dist big-box-dist) shape-dist))"
     :eng-program "(define (identity-under-enclosure? field)\n  ;; intersecting with a box far larger than the field yields a valid field\n  (let ((huge (sdf/box 160 120 400 400)))\n    (and (sdf? field) (sdf? (sdf-intersect field huge)))))\n(identity-under-enclosure? (sdf/circle 160 120 30))"
     :math-program "(define (max-commutes? x y) (= (max x y) (max y x)))\n;; for a point inside the shape and deep inside the enclosing box, max = shape distance\n(define (identity-at shape enclosing) (= (max shape enclosing) shape))\n(list 'commutes (max-commutes? -3 -1) 'identity (identity-at -4 -100))"
     :cs-program "(define (both-orders-field? a b)\n  (and (sdf? (sdf-intersect a b)) (sdf? (sdf-intersect b a))))\n(both-orders-field? (sdf/circle 160 120 40) (sdf/box 160 120 45 22))"
     :invariant "∀ a b. shape(sdf-intersect a b) = shape(sdf-intersect b a)  ∧  (box ⊇ A ⇒ shape(sdf-intersect A box) = A on box's interior)   (commutativity of max; a superset intersect is the identity)"
     :meta "The CS pattern is that intersection inherits its algebra from max: max is commutative, associative, and idempotent, so intersection is a commutative monoid whose identity is the everywhere-inside field (the whole plane, distance −∞). Intersecting with any superset therefore leaves the shape unchanged. Falsified if intersecting a shape with a box that fully contains it removed part of the shape — that would mean max drops a value that was already the maximum, contradicting the definition of max. book-of-math ch-advanced-04 (groups-symmetry) grounds the commutative-monoid structure; book-of-code ch 11 (the-alu) grounds max as a primitive commutative operation."
     )
    (:eng "The engineering consequence is that a designer can add a generous frame box to clip a scene to the canvas without fear of trimming the actual content — the frame is the identity everywhere except at the edges it is meant to cut." :math "(SDF, sdf-intersect) is a commutative monoid with identity = the whole-plane field; A ∩ U = A for any superset U." :cs "Commutative-associative reducer with an identity superset; the same algebra as AND over booleans where AND with true is the identity."))
  :row-5-emergence (

    (:base "sdf-intersect alone keeps only the overlap of two fields."
     :composition "(set-mode 'sd)\n;; a vesica / leaf: the lens where two equal circles overlap\n(let* ((c1 (sdf/circle 140 120 44))\n       (c2 (sdf/circle 180 120 44))\n       (leaf (sdf-intersect c1 c2)))\n  (sdf-fill leaf 11))"
     :eng-composition "(set-mode 'sd)\n(define (rounded-plate cx cy hw hh round color)\n  ;; operator build: a box AND a big circle = a rectangle with rounded corners\n  (let* ((body (sdf/box cx cy hw hh))\n         (rounder (sdf/circle cx cy round))\n         (plate (sdf-intersect body rounder)))\n    (sdf-fill plate color)))\n(rounded-plate 160 120 45 30 52 4)"
     :math-composition "(set-mode 'sd)\n;; a reuleaux-ish blob: intersect three big circles offset around a center\n(define c1 (sdf/circle 160 96 56))\n(define c2 (sdf/circle 132 144 56))\n(define c3 (sdf/circle 188 144 56))\n(sdf-fill (sdf-intersect (sdf-intersect c1 c2) c3) 12)"
     :cs-composition "(set-mode 'sd)\n;; clip a built-up union down to a viewport box — mask then paint\n(define scene (fold sdf-union (sdf/circle 100 120 30)\n                    (list (sdf/circle 160 120 30) (sdf/circle 220 120 30))))\n(define viewport (sdf/box 160 120 60 40))\n(sdf-fill (sdf-intersect scene viewport) 7)"
     :emergent "A vesica leaf, a rounded plate, a curved-triangle blob, and a clipped scene: intersection carves shapes down to the region several shapes share — masks, lenses, and rounded corners that only AND can make."
     :meta "This is the constructive-solid-geometry / masking family — intersection (pointwise max) as the AND that trims solids to their common region. Familiar analogs are clipping paths in vector graphics, masks in image editing, and set-intersection in region algebras. The composition works because intersection is a commutative monoid with a superset identity (Row 4), so masking a scene with a viewport removes only what falls outside, leaving the rest exactly. Grounds in book-of-code ch 05 (logic-gates-in-scheme) as the many-input-AND / mask-with-a-region shape."
     )
    (:eng "The audit lens is 'the viewport is a hard boundary' — intersecting a scene with a frame box guarantees nothing paints outside the frame, the SRE equivalent of a clip rectangle enforced in one field instead of a per-pixel bounds check." :math "A k-way intersection is the reduction of max over k fields, the lattice meet of k shapes; the surviving region is the sublevel set of the pointwise maximum." :cs "Reduce-with-a-commutative-monoid; the same shape as AND-ing a list of predicates, with (SDF, sdf-intersect) as the monoid and 'everywhere' as its identity."))
)

(verb
  :name "sdf-subtract" :std "Motoi"
  :library "sdf"
  :kind "meta"
  :signature "(sdf-subtract a b) -> {sdf}"
  :summary "Carve field b out of field a by taking max(a, -b), producing a field for the set difference A minus B."
  :explanation "Subtraction is the verb that makes SDFs feel like sculpting: it takes a bite out of a shape. To be inside A-minus-B a point must be inside A but OUTSIDE B — inside the block, but not inside the hole. The trick is that 'outside B' is exactly 'the NEGATION of B's field': flipping the sign of a signed distance turns inside into outside and vice versa. So the difference is max(f_a, -f_b): inside A (f_a ≤ 0) and outside B (−f_b ≤ 0, i.e. f_b ≥ 0). Unlike union and intersection, subtraction is NOT commutative — carving the hole out of the block is completely different from carving the block out of the hole. This is the primitive that makes rings, washers, keyholes, letters with counters (the hole in an O), and any shape defined by what has been removed from it."
  :examples (
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "novice" :code "(sdf? (sdf-subtract (sdf/circle 100 100 30) (sdf/circle 100 100 15)))" :note "Carve a small circle out of a big one: the result {sdf} is a ring (annulus) — the big disc minus the inner hole.")
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "intermediate" :code "(set-mode 'sd)\n(sdf-fill (sdf-subtract (sdf/box 160 120 40 40) (sdf/circle 160 120 18)) 6)" :note "A square plate with a round hole punched through its center — the box minus the circle, painted as one field.")
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "expert" :code "(define (bite plate x) (sdf-subtract plate (sdf/circle x 120 22)))\n(define plate (sdf/box 160 120 60 30))\n(sdf? (fold (lambda (x acc) (bite acc x)) plate (list 120 160 200)))" :note "Fold a series of circular bites out of a plate (element-first fold), each subtraction removing more material; the result is still one {sdf}.")
  )
  :caveats ("Subtraction is NOT commutative: (sdf-subtract a b) carves b out of a, while (sdf-subtract b a) carves a out of b — a completely different shape." "It computes max(a, -b), so the second argument is the hole and the first is the body; swapping them is the most common mistake." "Subtracting a shape from itself gives an empty field that paints nothing — occasionally useful, often a sign the arguments are wrong." "The −b negation makes max only an approximate distance near the carved edge, but the ≤ 0 fill region is exact.")
  :drawbacks ("Argument order matters, so it is easier to get wrong than the commutative union and intersect." "Over-subtracting can empty the shape entirely with no warning — a blank canvas that looks like a bug." "Each bite is another max evaluated per pixel, so a deeply carved shape costs more to sample.")
  :usecases ("Rings, washers, and annuli — a disc minus a smaller concentric disc." "A keyhole — a box minus a circle at the bottom." "Letter counters — the enclosed hole in an O, A, or D." "Windows and portholes cut into a wall or hull.")
  :related ("sdf-union" "sdf-intersect" "sdf/circle" "sdf/box" "sdf-fill")
  :learn (:concept "Set difference as max(a, -b): a point survives only if it is inside A and outside B, and negating B's field converts 'inside B' to 'outside'." :prerequisites ("sdf-intersect" "sdf/circle") :progression "Carve a small circle out of a big one to make a ring, then swap the arguments and watch the shape become nothing — the lesson that subtraction has a direction.")
  :row-2-audit (

    (:problem "A cart author is drawing a mechanical washer for a machine-parts scene: a solid disc with a clean round hole through the middle. Drawing a big disc and then a small background-colored disc over it fails the moment the washer sits on a textured background — the 'hole' is just a colored patch, not real emptiness."
     :program "(set-mode 'sd)\n(sdf-fill (sdf-subtract (sdf/circle 160 120 40) (sdf/circle 160 120 20)) 3)"
     :eng-program "(set-mode 'sd)\n(define (washer cx cy outer inner color)\n  ;; hardened: the hole is carved from the FIELD, so it is truly empty\n  (sdf-fill (sdf-subtract (sdf/circle cx cy outer) (sdf/circle cx cy inner)) color))\n(washer 160 120 40 20 3)"
     :math-program "(define (subtract-dist da db) (max da (- db)))\n;; inside A and OUTSIDE B: inside-A negative, -inside-B positive when outside B\n(list 'in-a-out-b (subtract-dist -5 6)   ;; kept\n      'in-a-in-b  (subtract-dist -5 -3)  ;; carved away (b's hole)\n      'out-a      (subtract-dist 4 -3))  ;; outside the body entirely"
     :cs-program "(define (carve body hole)\n  (if (and (sdf? body) (sdf? hole))\n      (sdf-subtract body hole)      ;; ORDER: body first, hole second\n      (error 'carve-needs-two-fields)))\n(sdf? (carve (sdf/circle 160 120 40) (sdf/circle 160 120 20)))"
     :explanation "eng: carve the hole from the field so it is real emptiness, not a colored patch. math: max(a,-b) keeps points inside A and outside B — the negation flips B's inside to outside. cs: a carve helper that fixes the body-then-hole argument order."
     :meta "sdf-union would ADD the inner circle, filling the hole instead of cutting it. sdf-intersect would keep only the inner disc, discarding the ring. Painting a background-colored disc over the body only masks pixels and breaks over any texture. sdf-subtract is the difference primitive: max(a,−b) removes B's region from A's field, so the hole is genuinely empty in the shape itself.")
    (:eng "The audit lens is 'is the hole real or painted-over?' — the smoke test is to place the shape on a busy background: a real subtracted hole shows the background through it, a painted patch does not." :math "The difference's field is max(f_a, −f_b); the ≤ 0 region is {p : f_a(p) ≤ 0 ∧ f_b(p) > 0} = A ∖ B." :cs "Pointwise max of a with the negation of b; O(1) per point, and the argument order (body, hole) is load-bearing."))
  :row-3-dimension (
    (:setup "Keep only what satisfies the first condition while FAILING the second — presence in A combined with absence from B."
     :rungs (
       (:domain "sdf-subtract"  :code "(sdf? (sdf-subtract (sdf/circle 100 100 30) (sdf/circle 100 100 15)))")
       (:domain "allow-minus-block" :code "(define (permitted? allowed? blocked?) (and allowed? (not blocked?)))\n(list (permitted? #t #f) (permitted? #t #t))")
       (:domain "set-difference" :code "(define (set-diff a b) (filter (lambda (x) (not (memv x b))) a))\n(set-diff '(1 2 3 4) '(2 4))"))
     :explanation "Rung 1: an SDF subtraction keeps points inside the body but outside the hole. Rung 2: an allow-list-minus-block-list gate permits a request only if it is on the allow-list AND not on the block-list. Rung 3: set difference A∖B keeps the elements of A that are not in B. All three share one shape — membership in A AND non-membership in B, i.e. A combined with the complement of B."
     :meta "The transfer works because subtraction is A ∩ ¬B — intersection with a complement. The complement is what makes it non-commutative: ¬B is not ¬A, so A∖B is not B∖A. On signed distances the complement is negation (max(a,−b)); on booleans it is (and a (not b)); on sets it is filter-out. Same A-minus-B shape, three encodings of complement. Grounds in book-of-code ch 05 (logic-gates-in-scheme) as the AND-NOT / difference shape."
     )
     (:eng "Every difference substrate shares one gotcha: 'which operand is the thing removed?' — the audit is to confirm the second argument is the exclusion (hole, block-list, subtrahend), because swapping them inverts the result." :math "A ∖ B = A ∩ B^c; on the shape lattice this is the meet of A with the complement of B, which is why it is order-sensitive." :cs "AND-NOT / set-minus; the same shape as allow-list minus block-list, SQL EXCEPT, and filtering out a exclusion set."))
  :row-4-proof (
    (:claim "Subtraction is NON-commutative, and subtracting a shape from itself yields the empty shape."
     :program "(define (subtract-dist da db) (max da (- db)))\n;; order matters: a-minus-b differs from b-minus-a at the same point\n(define p-a -5) (define p-b -2)  ;; a point inside both A and B\n(display (= (subtract-dist p-a p-b) (subtract-dist p-b p-a))) (newline)  ;; #f: not commutative\n;; self-subtraction: inside A and outside A is impossible -> empty (distance > 0 everywhere the shape was)\n(display (> (subtract-dist -5 -5) 0)) (newline)   ;; a point inside A is carved out by itself\n(display (> (subtract-dist -1 -1) 0))"
     :eng-program "(define (self-subtract-empty? field)\n  ;; carving a shape out of itself must produce a still-valid but empty field\n  (sdf? (sdf-subtract field field)))\n(self-subtract-empty? (sdf/circle 160 120 30))"
     :math-program "(define (subtract-dist da db) (max da (- db)))\n;; at any point inside A (da<0), self-subtract gives max(da, -da) = -da > 0 => outside\n(define (self-carve-sign da) (max da (- da)))\n(list (self-carve-sign -5) (self-carve-sign -1) (self-carve-sign 0))"
     :cs-program "(define (order-sensitive? a b)\n  ;; both orders are valid fields, but they are different shapes\n  (and (sdf? (sdf-subtract a b)) (sdf? (sdf-subtract b a))))\n(order-sensitive? (sdf/circle 140 120 30) (sdf/circle 180 120 30))"
     :invariant "∀ a b. shape(sdf-subtract a b) ≠ shape(sdf-subtract b a) in general  ∧  ∀ a. shape(sdf-subtract a a) = ∅   (subtraction is non-commutative; self-difference is empty)"
     :meta "The CS pattern is that subtraction is anti-symmetric where union and intersection are symmetric — it is A ∩ ¬B, and complementation breaks commutativity. The self-difference identity A∖A = ∅ falls out because max(f_a, −f_a) = |f_a| ≥ 0, so no point is strictly inside. Falsified if a-minus-b covered the same pixels as b-minus-a for two different overlapping shapes — that would mean max(a,−b) equals max(b,−a), which fails whenever a ≠ b. book-of-math ch-advanced-04 (groups-symmetry) grounds non-commutativity via the complement; book-of-code ch 05 (logic-gates-in-scheme) grounds AND-NOT."
     )
    (:eng "The engineering consequence is that a designer must always name the body first and the hole second; a swapped subtraction is the single most common CSG bug, and the self-subtract-is-empty rule is the fastest sanity check that the arguments are distinct." :math "sdf-subtract implements A ∩ B^c; because B^c ≠ A^c in general, the operation is not commutative, and A ∩ A^c = ∅." :cs "Non-commutative AND-NOT; the same shape as SQL 'A EXCEPT B' where reversing the tables gives a different result set."))
  :row-5-emergence (

    (:base "sdf-subtract alone carves one hole out of one body."
     :composition "(set-mode 'sd)\n;; a keyhole: a box body with a round hole near the top and a slot below\n(let* ((body (sdf/box 160 120 22 40))\n       (hole (sdf/circle 160 100 12))\n       (slot (sdf/box 160 135 4 20))\n       (keyhole (sdf-subtract (sdf-subtract body hole) slot)))\n  (sdf-fill keyhole 12))"
     :eng-composition "(set-mode 'sd)\n(define (crescent cx cy r offset color)\n  ;; operator build: a moon is a disc minus a disc shifted sideways\n  (let* ((full (sdf/circle cx cy r))\n         (shadow (sdf/circle (+ cx offset) cy r))\n         (moon (sdf-subtract full shadow)))\n    (sdf-fill moon color)))\n(crescent 160 120 44 22 7)"
     :math-composition "(set-mode 'sd)\n;; a gear/washer: (box OR circle) MINUS a center hole MINUS a keyway slot\n(define hub (sdf-union (sdf/box 160 120 28 28) (sdf/circle 160 120 34)))\n(define bored (sdf-subtract hub (sdf/circle 160 120 12)))\n(define keyway (sdf-subtract bored (sdf/box 160 84 5 12)))\n(sdf-fill keyway 3)"
     :cs-composition "(set-mode 'sd)\n;; fold a list of holes out of one plate: each subtraction is one carve step\n(define plate (sdf/box 160 120 70 26))\n(define holes (map (lambda (x) (sdf/circle x 120 12)) (list 110 145 180 215)))\n(sdf-fill (fold (lambda (h acc) (sdf-subtract acc h)) plate holes) 6)"
     :emergent "A keyhole, a crescent moon, a gear-washer with a keyway, and a perforated plate: subtraction carves recognizable objects DEFINED BY THEIR HOLES — shapes that exist only because material was removed."
     :meta "This is the constructive-solid-geometry family in its sculpting mode — subtraction (max with a negated field) as the drill and chisel that define a shape by what is absent. Familiar analogs are pocket-and-bore operations in CAD/CAM, boolean-difference modeling in 3-D packages, and knockout masks in compositing. The composition works because each carve keeps the result an {sdf} (Row 4), so holes fold cleanly one after another, and the body-first/hole-second order (non-commutative, Row 4) makes each bite land exactly where intended. Grounds in book-of-code ch 05 (logic-gates-in-scheme) as the repeated-AND-NOT / knockout shape."
     )
    (:eng "The audit lens is 'each hole is a carve step, order-sensitive' — folding subtractions over a plate means the SRE builds one field with every hole baked in, one paint call, and the fold's accumulator must stay the body (first arg) at every step or the bites invert." :math "A body with k holes is body ∩ hole₁^c ∩ … ∩ hole_k^c, realized as a fold of max-with-negation; the surviving region is the body minus the union of the holes." :cs "Left-fold of a non-commutative AND-NOT with the body as accumulator; the same shape as applying a sequence of knockout masks in order."))
)

(verb
  :name "sdf?" :std "Motoi"
  :library "sdf"
  :kind "meta"
  :signature "(sdf? v) -> boolean"
  :summary "Return #t if v is a signed distance field value, #f for anything else."
  :explanation "sdf? is the type predicate for the whole library — the one verb that does not build or paint anything, but simply answers 'is this thing an {sdf}?' Because every constructor (circle, box, segment) and every boolean (union, intersect, subtract) returns an {sdf}, and because the fill sinks EXPECT an {sdf}, this predicate is how you tell fields apart from the numbers, lists, and symbols that flow through the same code. It returns a strict boolean: #t for a genuine field, #f for an integer, a string, a list of points, a color symbol, or anything else. Use it as a guard before painting, as a smoke test after building, and as the membership check that proves the whole CSG pipeline stays inside its type — build a circle, carve it, union it, and sdf? still answers #t at every step."
  :examples (
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "novice" :code "(sdf? (sdf/circle 100 100 30))" :note "The canonical use: confirm that a freshly built circle field really is an {sdf} — the answer is #t.")
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "intermediate" :code "(list (sdf? (sdf/box 100 100 20 20))\n      (sdf? 42)\n      (sdf? (list 1 2 3))\n      (sdf? 'red))" :note "Only the first element is a field; the integer, the list of numbers, and the color symbol all answer #f, showing sdf? distinguishes fields from ordinary data.")
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "expert" :code "(define pipeline (sdf-subtract (sdf-union (sdf/circle 100 100 30) (sdf/box 130 100 20 20))\n                               (sdf/circle 115 100 10)))\n(sdf? pipeline)" :note "A field that has been unioned then carved is still an {sdf}: sdf? proves the CSG operations never leave the type.")
  )
  :caveats ("It only checks the TYPE, not whether the field is non-empty or well-formed geometrically; an empty field (e.g. from self-subtraction) still answers #t." "A list of points or a raster image is NOT an {sdf}; sdf? correctly answers #f for both." "It returns a strict boolean (#t or #f), so it is safe to use directly in and/or/cond without truthiness surprises." "It does not evaluate the field at any point; it is a constant-time tag check, not a distance computation.")
  :drawbacks ("It cannot tell you whether the field will paint anything — an empty-but-valid field passes." "It does not distinguish a circle field from a box field or a carved field; all of them are just {sdf} to this predicate." "As a pure tag check it gives no geometric information — for that you must actually sample or paint the field.")
  :usecases ("Guarding a paint call: only sdf-fill a value once sdf? confirms it is a field." "Smoke-testing a constructor or boolean in a test: (sdf? (sdf-union a b)) should be #t." "Filtering a mixed list down to only the fields before folding a union." "Documenting and enforcing the {sdf} contract at the boundary of a helper function.")
  :related ("sdf/circle" "sdf/box" "sdf/segment" "sdf-union" "sdf-fill")
  :learn (:concept "A type predicate as a membership test for a type: sdf? is the characteristic function of the {sdf} type, mapping any value to #t or #f." :prerequisites ("sdf/circle") :progression "Use sdf? to check a constructor, then a boolean, then a whole carved pipeline — watch it stay #t throughout, proving the type is closed under every operation in this library.")
  :row-2-audit (

    (:problem "A helper function accepts 'a shape' from a caller and paints it, but callers sometimes pass a list of points or a color index by mistake, and the paint call then fails deep inside with a confusing error. The author wants the helper to reject non-fields at the boundary with a clear message."
     :program "(define (paint-shape v color)\n  (if (sdf? v)\n      (begin (set-mode 'sd) (sdf-fill v color))\n      (list 'error 'not-a-field v)))\n(paint-shape (list 1 2 3) 7)"
     :eng-program "(define (safe-paint v color)\n  ;; hardened boundary: reject non-fields with a specific diagnostic instead of a deep crash\n  (cond ((not (sdf? v)) (list 'reject 'expected-sdf-got v))\n        (else (begin (set-mode 'sd) (sdf-fill v color) 'painted))))\n(list (safe-paint (list 1 2 3) 7)          ;; a non-field is rejected with a clear tag\n      (safe-paint (sdf/circle 160 120 30) 7))  ;; a real field paints and reports 'painted"
     :math-program "(define (is-field-membership v) (if (sdf? v) 1 0))  ;; characteristic function -> {0,1}\n(list (is-field-membership (sdf/circle 100 100 20))\n      (is-field-membership 42)\n      (is-field-membership 'red))"
     :cs-program "(define (only-fields vs) (filter sdf? vs))\n;; keep exactly the {sdf} values out of a mixed list\n(length (only-fields (list (sdf/circle 100 100 20) 42 (sdf/box 100 100 10 10) 'red \"x\")))"
     :explanation "eng: guard the paint boundary and reject non-fields with a named error. math: sdf? is a characteristic function mapping values to {0,1} membership. cs: filter a mixed list by sdf? to keep only the fields."
     :meta "There is no sibling for this job: sdf-fill assumes its argument is already a field and errors obscurely otherwise, the constructors build fields but cannot test an arbitrary value, and number?/list? test the wrong types. sdf? is the only verb that answers 'is this an {sdf}?', which is exactly the guard a defensive helper needs at its boundary.")
    (:eng "The audit lens is 'guard before you paint' — the SRE fix for an obscure deep failure is a sdf? check at the function boundary that converts a late crash into an early, specific rejection." :math "sdf? is the characteristic function 1_SDF : Value → {⊤, ⊥} of the {sdf} type; membership is decidable in constant time." :cs "A constant-time tag check; the same shape as pair?, number?, or any runtime type predicate that inspects a value's tag without traversing it."))
  :row-3-dimension (
    (:setup "Ask a yes/no question about a value's KIND before any operation that only makes sense for that kind."
     :rungs (
       (:domain "sdf-predicate"  :code "(list (sdf? (sdf/circle 100 100 20)) (sdf? 42))")
       (:domain "form-validation" :code "(define (valid-quantity? v) (and (number? v) (> v 0)))\n(list (valid-quantity? 5) (valid-quantity? -1) (valid-quantity? 'x))")
       (:domain "characteristic-function" :code "(define (in-set? s x) (if (memv x s) #t #f))\n(list (in-set? '(1 2 3) 2) (in-set? '(1 2 3) 9))"))
     :explanation "Rung 1: sdf? asks whether a value belongs to the {sdf} type. Rung 2: a form validator asks whether an input belongs to the set of acceptable quantities before it hits the backend. Rung 3: a characteristic function asks whether an element belongs to a set, returning #t or #f. All three share one shape — a decidable membership test that returns a boolean, gating whatever operation only makes sense for members."
     :meta "The transfer works because a type predicate IS a characteristic function of a set — the set of values of that type. Every substrate that must decide 'does this belong before I proceed?' — a form validator, a route guard, a type check — is computing a characteristic function. Grounds in book-of-code ch 05 (logic-gates-in-scheme) as the predicate / membership-test shape."
     )
     (:eng "Every membership-test substrate shares one probe: 'does it return exactly #t or #f, never a truthy-but-not-#t value?' — a strict boolean is what makes it safe to compose in and/or/cond." :math "A characteristic function 1_S : X → {⊤, ⊥} for a set S ⊆ X; here S is the {sdf} type and the test is decidable in O(1)." :cs "A runtime type predicate / membership test; the same shape as pair?, string?, or a set-membership check on a bitset."))
  :row-4-proof (
    (:claim "sdf? is a total, closed predicate: it answers #t for every value the library's constructors and booleans produce, and #f for every ordinary datum."
     :program "(display (sdf? (sdf/circle 100 100 30))) (newline)\n(display (sdf? (sdf/box 100 100 20 20))) (newline)\n(display (sdf? (sdf/segment 20 20 100 100 4))) (newline)\n(display (sdf? (sdf-union (sdf/circle 100 100 20) (sdf/box 130 100 15 15)))) (newline)\n(display (sdf? (sdf-subtract (sdf/circle 100 100 30) (sdf/circle 100 100 15)))) (newline)\n(display (sdf? 42)) (newline)\n(display (sdf? (list 1 2 3)))"
     :eng-program "(define (all-constructors-are-fields?)\n  (fold (lambda (v acc) (and acc (sdf? v))) #t\n        (list (sdf/circle 100 100 20)\n              (sdf/box 100 100 20 20)\n              (sdf/segment 10 10 90 90 4)\n              (sdf-union (sdf/circle 100 100 20) (sdf/box 130 100 15 15))\n              (sdf-intersect (sdf/circle 100 100 20) (sdf/circle 120 100 20))\n              (sdf-subtract (sdf/circle 100 100 30) (sdf/circle 100 100 15)))))\n(all-constructors-are-fields?)"
     :math-program "(define (no-nonfield-passes?)\n  ;; the characteristic function must be 0 on every non-field sample\n  (fold (lambda (v acc) (and acc (not (sdf? v)))) #t\n        (list 0 42 -3.5 'red 'circle (list 1 2) \"box\" #t #f)))\n(no-nonfield-passes?)"
     :cs-program "(define (strict-boolean? v) (or (eq? v #t) (eq? v #f)))\n;; the predicate's OUTPUT must itself be a strict boolean\n(and (strict-boolean? (sdf? (sdf/circle 100 100 20)))\n     (strict-boolean? (sdf? 42)))"
     :invariant "∀ v produced by an sdf constructor or boolean. sdf?(v) = #t   ∧   ∀ ordinary datum d (number, list, symbol, string, boolean). sdf?(d) = #f"
     :meta "The CS pattern is soundness-and-completeness of a type predicate: it accepts exactly the values of its type (every constructor output) and rejects everything else, and the whole library's type-closure (every boolean returns a field) means the predicate stays #t through any pipeline. Falsified either way — if a real field answered #f the paint guards would reject valid work, and if a plain number answered #t the guards would admit garbage into sdf-fill. book-of-code ch 05 (logic-gates-in-scheme) grounds the sound-and-complete predicate; book-of-math ch-advanced-04 (groups-symmetry) grounds closure of a type under its operations."
     )
    (:eng "The engineering consequence is that a single sdf? guard at a boundary is trustworthy: because the type is closed, a value that passes the guard will still be a field after any number of unions and carves, so downstream code need not re-check." :math "sdf? is the indicator of a set closed under the library's operations; closure means the indicator is preserved by every combinator." :cs "A sound-and-complete type predicate over a closed type; the same guarantee as pair? holding through cons/car/cdr chains that stay in the pair type."))
  :row-5-emergence (

    (:base "sdf? alone answers whether one value is a field."
     :composition "(set-mode 'sd)\n;; a defensive painter: only fields get painted, non-fields are skipped and reported\n(define (paint-all items color)\n  (for-each (lambda (v) (when (sdf? v) (sdf-fill v color))) items)\n  (filter (lambda (v) (not (sdf? v))) items))  ;; return the rejected items\n(paint-all (list (sdf/circle 100 120 24) 42 (sdf/box 200 120 20 20) 'oops) 7)"
     :eng-composition "(set-mode 'sd)\n(define (paint-fields-only items color)\n  ;; operator build: filter to fields, union them, paint once; report the rejects\n  (let ((fields (filter sdf? items)))\n    (if (null? fields) 'nothing-to-paint\n        (begin (sdf-fill (fold sdf-union (car fields) (cdr fields)) color)\n               (list 'painted (length fields) 'skipped (- (length items) (length fields)))))))\n(paint-fields-only (list (sdf/circle 110 120 22) 'x (sdf/circle 170 120 22) 99 (sdf/circle 230 120 22)) 12)"
     :math-composition "(define (field-count items) (length (filter sdf? items)))\n;; count how many items pass the characteristic function\n(field-count (list (sdf/circle 100 100 20) 42 (sdf/box 100 100 10 10) 'red (sdf/segment 10 10 90 90 4)))"
     :cs-composition "(define (partition-fields items)\n  ;; split a mixed list into fields and non-fields by the predicate\n  (list 'fields (filter sdf? items)\n        'others (filter (lambda (v) (not (sdf? v))) items)))\n(let ((r (partition-fields (list (sdf/circle 100 100 20) 42 (sdf/box 100 100 10 10) 'red))))\n  (list (length (cadr r)) (length (cadddr r))))"
     :emergent "A defensive rendering pipeline: a mixed bag of values is partitioned by sdf?, the fields are unioned and painted in one sweep, and the non-fields are reported instead of crashing — a type-safe painter built on one predicate."
     :meta "This is the guard / validate-then-process family — a predicate partitions inputs so the expensive, type-specific stage only ever sees valid members. Familiar analogs are filtering a request stream by a validity predicate before handling, partitioning a heterogeneous list before dispatch, and instanceof-guards before a downcast. The composition works because sdf? is sound and complete (Row 4), so filtering by it yields exactly the paintable values — no valid field dropped, no garbage admitted. Grounds in book-of-code ch 05 (logic-gates-in-scheme) as the filter-by-predicate-then-process shape."
     )
    (:eng "The audit lens is 'reject at the boundary, not in the sink' — filtering by sdf? before folding a union means sdf-fill never receives a non-field, so the SRE sees clean rejections and a report instead of an obscure paint crash." :math "The pipeline is (filter 1_SDF) then reduce-with-union then paint; the predicate's soundness guarantees the reduced field is well-typed." :cs "Filter-by-type-predicate-then-process; the same shape as validating a list before a bulk operation, with sdf? as the type guard."))
)

(verb
  :name "sdf-fill" :std "Motoi"
  :library "sdf"
  :kind "meta"
  :signature "(sdf-fill sdf [color]) -> undefined"
  :summary "Sample the whole canvas and paint every pixel where the field's signed distance is <= 0, using the given color."
  :explanation "This is the verb that turns a field into pixels — the bridge from the abstract distance function to something you can see. sdf-fill walks every pixel of the 320×240 SD canvas, evaluates the field's f(x,y) at that pixel, and paints it in the chosen color when f(x,y) ≤ 0 (inside-or-on the shape), skipping it otherwise. That single ≤ 0 test is where all the CSG arithmetic finally cashes out: whatever unions, intersections, and subtractions you did to build the field, sdf-fill just asks 'is this pixel inside?' and colors accordingly. It PAINTS and returns undefined, so like every sink it requires (set-mode 'sd) first and prints nothing — success is a clean run and a shape on the framebuffer. The optional color is a palette index 0–15 or a color-name symbol; omit it to use the current color."
  :examples (
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "novice" :code "(set-mode 'sd)\n(sdf-fill (sdf/circle 160 120 40) 12)" :note "Paint a solid disc: every pixel within radius 40 of the center is inside (f <= 0) and gets color 12.")
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "intermediate" :code "(set-mode 'sd)\n(sdf-fill (sdf-subtract (sdf/box 160 120 40 40) (sdf/circle 160 120 16)) 'cyan)" :note "Paint a carved shape with a named color: the box-minus-circle field is sampled and every inside pixel is painted cyan.")
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "expert" :code "(set-mode 'sd)\n(define blob (fold sdf-union (sdf/circle 130 120 26)\n                   (list (sdf/circle 160 100 28) (sdf/circle 190 120 26))))\n(sdf-fill blob 7)" :note "Build a compound field by folding a union, then paint the whole silhouette in one canvas sweep.")
  )
  :caveats ("It PAINTS and returns undefined; it MUST be preceded by (set-mode 'sd) or there is no framebuffer to paint into." "It samples the ENTIRE canvas, so painting one small shape still visits every pixel — use sdf-fill-rect to bound the work." "The fill region is f(x,y) <= 0 (inside-or-on); a field that is empty everywhere (e.g. self-subtraction) paints nothing, which is not an error." "The optional color is a palette index 0–15 or a color-name symbol; an unknown name falls back to the current color.")
  :drawbacks ("Whole-canvas sampling is the slowest way to paint a small shape; the cost is fixed at 320×240 evaluations regardless of shape size." "It paints a flat fill only — no outline, no anti-aliasing, no gradient; the boundary is a hard pixel edge." "It gives no feedback about whether anything was painted; an empty field and a filled one both return undefined.")
  :usecases ("Painting a finished CSG field — the final step after all unions, intersections, and subtractions." "Filling a large or full-canvas shape where the bounding box is most of the canvas anyway." "Quick previews while iterating on a field, before optimizing with sdf-fill-rect." "Rendering a background or a shape that genuinely spans the whole scene.")
  :related ("sdf-fill-rect" "sdf/circle" "sdf-union" "sdf-subtract" "set-mode")
  :learn (:concept "Rasterizing an implicit shape: sample the field over the pixel grid and paint where signed distance is <= 0, turning a function into an image." :prerequisites ("set-mode" "sdf/circle") :progression "Fill a single circle, then fill a carved field, then switch to sdf-fill-rect once you notice full-canvas sampling is wasteful for small shapes.")
  :row-2-audit (

    (:problem "A cart author has finished building a compound field — a snowman unioned from three discs — and now needs it on the screen. They are not sure which verb actually draws it, since the constructors and booleans all returned values that painted nothing."
     :program "(set-mode 'sd)\n(define snowman (fold sdf-union (sdf/circle 160 180 30)\n                      (list (sdf/circle 160 135 24) (sdf/circle 160 100 16))))\n(sdf-fill snowman 7)"
     :eng-program "(set-mode 'sd)\n(define (render field color)\n  ;; operator build: one entry point that guards the field then paints\n  (if (sdf? field) (sdf-fill field color) (error 'render-needs-a-field field)))\n(render (fold sdf-union (sdf/circle 160 180 30)\n              (list (sdf/circle 160 135 24) (sdf/circle 160 100 16))) 7)"
     :math-program "(define (inside-count-estimate field-inside? w h)\n  ;; the fill's work is one <=0 test per pixel over the w*h grid\n  (list 'pixels-visited (* w h) 'rule 'paint-if-f-le-0))\n(inside-count-estimate 'field 320 240)"
     :cs-program "(set-mode 'sd)\n(define (fill-guarded field color)\n  (cond ((not (sdf? field)) (error 'not-a-field field))\n        (else (sdf-fill field color) 'ok)))\n(fill-guarded (sdf/circle 160 120 40) 12)"
     :explanation "eng: one guarded render entry point so a non-field fails clearly. math: the fill visits every pixel once and paints on the f <= 0 test. cs: a guard that confirms sdf? before committing to the sink."
     :meta "The constructors (circle/box/segment) and booleans (union/intersect/subtract) all return fields and paint nothing — they build the description. sdf-fill-rect paints too but only within a bounding rectangle. sdf-fill is the whole-canvas rasterizer: the verb that finally samples the field over every pixel and commits the shape to the framebuffer.")
    (:eng "The audit lens is 'did set-mode run first, and is the argument a field?' — the two-part smoke test before any fill; miss set-mode and there is no framebuffer, pass a non-field and the sink fails obscurely." :math "The fill is the rasterization of the sublevel set {p : f(p) ≤ 0} onto the discrete pixel grid; each pixel is one membership test." :cs "A full-grid rasterizer; O(W·H) field evaluations, one per pixel, independent of the shape's size."))
  :row-3-dimension (
    (:setup "Materialize a function into a concrete table by evaluating it at every point of a fixed grid and recording the result."
     :rungs (
       (:domain "sdf-fill"      :code "(set-mode 'sd)\n(sdf-fill (sdf/circle 160 120 40) 12)")
       (:domain "heatmap-render" :code "(define (heatmap f w h) (map (lambda (y) (map (lambda (x) (f x y)) (iota w))) (iota h)))\n(length (heatmap (lambda (x y) (+ x y)) 3 2))")
       (:domain "function-table" :code "(define (tabulate f xs) (map (lambda (x) (list x (f x))) xs))\n(tabulate (lambda (x) (* x x)) '(0 1 2 3))"))
     :explanation "Rung 1: sdf-fill evaluates a field at every pixel of the canvas grid and paints per the result. Rung 2: a heatmap renderer evaluates a scalar function at every cell of a grid and colors each cell by its value. Rung 3: tabulating a function samples it at a list of inputs and records each (x, f(x)) pair. All three share one shape — take a function over a domain and materialize it into a concrete grid or table by evaluating it everywhere."
     :meta "The transfer works because rasterizing IS sampling a function on a fixed grid. Whether the output is a painted pixel, a heatmap cell, or a table row, the shape is discretize-the-domain-then-evaluate-everywhere. Grounds in book-of-math ch-advanced-10 (numerical-methods) as the grid-sampling / discretization shape."
     )
     (:eng "Every grid-sampling substrate shares the same cost law — work is proportional to grid size, not to the interesting region — and the SRE lens is 'bound the grid to the region of interest when you can'." :math "Discretization of a function f : ℝ² → ℝ onto a pixel lattice; the painted set is the discrete approximation of the continuous sublevel set." :cs "The evaluate-on-a-grid pattern; the same shape as rendering a heatmap, filling a Z-buffer, or tabulating a function over a mesh."))
  :row-4-proof (
    (:claim "The fill region is exactly the sublevel set f <= 0: a pixel is painted iff the field's signed distance there is non-positive, so the boolean built into the field decides the pixel."
     :program "(define (union-dist da db) (min da db))\n(define (paint? d) (<= d 0))\n;; a pixel inside either unioned shape has min-distance <= 0 -> painted\n(display (paint? (union-dist -3 5))) (newline)   ;; inside A: painted\n(display (paint? (union-dist 4 -2))) (newline)   ;; inside B: painted\n(display (paint? (union-dist 6 8))) (newline)    ;; outside both: not painted\n(display (paint? (union-dist 0 9)))              ;; exactly on A's edge: painted"
     :eng-program "(set-mode 'sd)\n(define (fill-matches-membership? cx cy r)\n  ;; the fill paints the same set the field's sign predicts; both run clean\n  (let ((field (sdf/circle cx cy r)))\n    (sdf-fill field 12)\n    (sdf? field)))\n(fill-matches-membership? 160 120 40)"
     :math-program "(define (membership f-value) (<= f-value 0))\n;; the painted set is {p : f(p) <= 0}, boundary included at f = 0\n(list 'inside (membership -2) 'boundary (membership 0) 'outside (membership 3))"
     :cs-program "(define (threshold-is-le-zero? sample)\n  ;; the paint predicate is exactly (<= sample 0), not (< sample 0)\n  (eq? (<= sample 0) (paint-rule sample)))\n(define (paint-rule s) (<= s 0))\n(and (threshold-is-le-zero? -1) (threshold-is-le-zero? 0) (threshold-is-le-zero? 2))"
     :invariant "∀ field. ∀ pixel p. painted(p) ⟺ f_field(p) ≤ 0   (the fill paints exactly the closed sublevel set, boundary included)"
     :meta "The CS pattern is that the sink faithfully renders the field's own membership test — the fill adds no geometry of its own, it only thresholds at zero. Falsified if a pixel where f > 0 got painted (fill leaking outside the shape) or a pixel where f ≤ 0 was skipped (fill dropping the boundary) — either would mean the rasterizer and the field disagree about what 'inside' means. book-of-code ch 05 (logic-gates-in-scheme) grounds the threshold predicate; book-of-math ch-advanced-06 (analytic-geometry) grounds the closed sublevel set."
     )
    (:eng "The engineering consequence is that everything about WHICH pixels paint lives in the field, not the fill — to change the shape you change the CSG, and the fill will render it faithfully without a code change." :math "sdf-fill is the indicator of the closed sublevel set {p : f(p) ≤ 0}; the boundary f = 0 is included." :cs "A threshold-at-zero rasterizer; the same shape as thresholding any scalar field to a binary mask at a fixed cutoff."))
  :row-5-emergence (

    (:base "sdf-fill paints one field over the whole canvas."
     :composition "(set-mode 'sd)\n;; a moon in a night sky: fill a big background box, then a crescent on top\n(sdf-fill (sdf/box 160 120 160 120) 1)\n(sdf-fill (sdf-subtract (sdf/circle 180 100 40) (sdf/circle 200 96 40)) 10)"
     :eng-composition "(set-mode 'sd)\n(define (scene layers)\n  ;; operator build: paint an ordered list of (field . color) back to front\n  (for-each (lambda (l) (sdf-fill (car l) (cdr l))) layers))\n(scene (list (cons (sdf/box 160 120 160 120) 1)\n             (cons (sdf/circle 160 190 60) 3)\n             (cons (sdf-subtract (sdf/circle 190 90 34) (sdf/circle 208 86 34)) 10)))"
     :math-composition "(set-mode 'sd)\n;; layered fills compose by z-order: later fills paint over earlier ones\n(define bg (sdf/box 160 120 160 120))\n(define hill (sdf/circle 160 210 80))\n(sdf-fill bg 1)\n(sdf-fill hill 3)"
     :cs-composition "(set-mode 'sd)\n;; a painter's-algorithm loop: fold over layers, each a paint side-effect\n(define layers (list (cons (sdf/box 160 120 160 120) 1)\n                     (cons (sdf/circle 100 120 26) 12)\n                     (cons (sdf/circle 220 120 26) 12)))\n(for-each (lambda (l) (sdf-fill (car l) (cdr l))) layers)"
     :emergent "A layered scene: a background box, a hill, and a crescent moon are filled back-to-front, later fills painting over earlier ones — a whole picture composed by z-ordered sink calls, the painter's algorithm."
     :meta "This is the compositing / painter's-algorithm family — successive fills layered by draw order to build a scene, distinct from the CSG that builds each individual field. Familiar analogs are z-ordered draw calls in a 2-D engine, layer stacks in an image editor, and back-to-front rendering of transparent geometry. The composition works because each fill is a self-contained sink that paints exactly its field's sublevel set (Row 4), so ordering the fills orders the layers — the field algebra builds the shapes, the fill order builds the scene. Grounds in book-of-code ch 05 (logic-gates-in-scheme) as the ordered-side-effects / layering shape."
     )
    (:eng "The audit lens is 'draw order is z-order' — the SRE rule is background first, foreground last, because each fill overwrites the pixels it covers; a wrong order buries the subject behind the backdrop." :math "The final image is the ordered composition of indicator fills; pixel color is the last layer whose sublevel set covers it." :cs "Painter's algorithm over a layer list; the same shape as back-to-front blitting, with each fill a full-canvas paint side-effect."))
)

(verb
  :name "sdf-fill-rect" :std "Motoi"
  :library "sdf"
  :kind "meta"
  :signature "(sdf-fill-rect sdf x0 y0 x1 y1 [color]) -> undefined"
  :summary "Sample only the (x0,y0)-(x1,y1) rectangle and paint pixels there where the field is <= 0 — a bounded, faster sdf-fill."
  :explanation "sdf-fill-rect does exactly what sdf-fill does — paint every pixel where the field is ≤ 0 — but only within the rectangle you name, instead of the whole canvas. That restriction is the entire point: if your shape lives in a corner, sampling all 320×240 pixels to paint it is wasteful, and bounding the sample region to the shape's bounding box can cut the work by an order of magnitude. The (x0,y0)-(x1,y1) rectangle is the sample window; pixels outside it are never evaluated and never touched, so it also acts as a clip. Like sdf-fill it PAINTS, returns undefined, and requires (set-mode 'sd) first. The optional color is the same palette-index-or-name as sdf-fill. Reach for this verb once a scene has many small shapes and whole-canvas fills start to cost you frames — it is the performance-conscious sibling of sdf-fill."
  :examples (
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "novice" :code "(set-mode 'sd)\n(sdf-fill-rect (sdf/circle 100 100 20) 60 60 140 140 8)" :note "Paint a small disc but only sample the 60,60-140,140 box around it, so pixels far from the shape are never visited.")
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "intermediate" :code "(set-mode 'sd)\n(sdf-fill-rect (sdf-subtract (sdf/box 100 100 30 30) (sdf/circle 100 100 12)) 60 60 140 140 12)" :note "A carved plate painted within its own bounding box: the box-minus-circle field is sampled only where it can be non-empty.")
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "expert" :code "(set-mode 'sd)\n(define (paint-in-bbox field cx cy r color)\n  (sdf-fill-rect field (- cx r) (- cy r) (+ cx r) (+ cy r) color))\n(paint-in-bbox (sdf/circle 220 60 24) 220 60 24 11)" :note "Compute a shape's bounding box from its center and radius and pass it as the sample window, so the fill cost scales with the shape, not the canvas.")
  )
  :caveats ("It PAINTS and returns undefined; it MUST be preceded by (set-mode 'sd)." "Pixels OUTSIDE the rectangle are never sampled, so if the rectangle is too small it will CLIP the shape — make the window at least the shape's bounding box." "The rectangle is a sample window, not a second shape; it does not round or intersect, it just bounds where sampling happens." "The optional color is a palette index 0–15 or a color-name symbol; omit it for the current color.")
  :drawbacks ("A too-tight rectangle silently clips the shape — the most common bug, and there is no warning." "You must know or compute the shape's bounding box; for a complex carved field that is not always obvious." "For a shape that genuinely fills the canvas there is no speedup over sdf-fill — the rectangle is the whole canvas anyway.")
  :usecases ("Painting many small shapes in a scene without paying whole-canvas cost per shape." "Redrawing just the region around a moving sprite each frame instead of the whole canvas." "Clipping a fill to a known region of interest while also saving sampling work." "Any inner-loop paint where the shape is much smaller than the canvas.")
  :related ("sdf-fill" "sdf/circle" "sdf/box" "sdf-subtract" "set-mode")
  :learn (:concept "Bounded rasterization: restrict field sampling to a rectangle so cost scales with the region of interest instead of the whole grid." :prerequisites ("sdf-fill" "sdf/box") :progression "Paint a small shape with sdf-fill, then repaint it with sdf-fill-rect bounded to its bounding box and note the identical result for less work — then feel the clipping bug by shrinking the window too far.")
  :row-2-audit (

    (:problem "A cart runs a scene with a dozen small gauges, each a carved circle, repainted every frame. Using sdf-fill for each one samples the full 320×240 canvas twelve times per frame and the frame rate suffers, even though each gauge occupies a tiny patch of screen."
     :program "(set-mode 'sd)\n(define (gauge cx cy)\n  (sdf-fill-rect (sdf-subtract (sdf/circle cx cy 18) (sdf/circle cx cy 8))\n                 (- cx 20) (- cy 20) (+ cx 20) (+ cy 20) 11))\n(gauge 80 80)\n(gauge 160 80)\n(gauge 240 80)"
     :eng-program "(set-mode 'sd)\n(define (bounded-paint field cx cy r color)\n  ;; hardened: derive the window from center+radius so it never clips\n  (sdf-fill-rect field (- cx r) (- cy r) (+ cx r) (+ cy r) color))\n(bounded-paint (sdf-subtract (sdf/circle 80 80 18) (sdf/circle 80 80 8)) 80 80 18 11)"
     :math-program "(define (rect-area x0 y0 x1 y1) (* (- x1 x0) (- y1 y0)))\n;; cost ratio: window pixels vs whole-canvas pixels\n(list 'window (rect-area 60 60 100 100) 'canvas (* 320 240)\n      'speedup (/ (* 320 240) (rect-area 60 60 100 100)))"
     :cs-program "(set-mode 'sd)\n(define (safe-window-paint field x0 y0 x1 y1 color)\n  (if (and (< x0 x1) (< y0 y1))\n      (sdf-fill-rect field x0 y0 x1 y1 color)\n      (error 'degenerate-window x0 y0 x1 y1)))\n(safe-window-paint (sdf/circle 100 100 20) 60 60 140 140 8)"
     :explanation "eng: derive the sample window from center+radius so it always covers the shape. math: the speedup is the ratio of canvas pixels to window pixels. cs: a guard rejecting a degenerate (inverted or empty) window before painting."
     :meta "sdf-fill paints the same shape but samples the whole canvas — correct but wasteful for a small gauge. The constructors and booleans build the field but never paint. sdf-fill-rect is the bounded rasterizer: identical fill rule, but the sample window scales the cost to the shape, which is exactly what a per-frame inner loop of small shapes needs.")
    (:eng "The audit lens is 'is the window at least the bounding box?' — the smoke test after any sdf-fill-rect is to widen the window and confirm the shape does not grow; if it does, the tighter window was clipping." :math "The painted set is {p ∈ rect : f(p) ≤ 0}; the rectangle intersects the sublevel set with a bounding window." :cs "A windowed rasterizer; O((x1−x0)·(y1−y0)) evaluations instead of O(W·H), a constant-factor win proportional to the area ratio."))
  :row-3-dimension (
    (:setup "Do the same per-element work as a full pass, but only over a bounded sub-region so cost tracks the region instead of the whole domain."
     :rungs (
       (:domain "sdf-fill-rect"  :code "(set-mode 'sd)\n(sdf-fill-rect (sdf/circle 100 100 20) 60 60 140 140 8)")
       (:domain "dirty-rectangle" :code "(define (redraw-region x0 y0 x1 y1) (list 'repaint-only (* (- x1 x0) (- y1 y0)) 'pixels))\n(redraw-region 60 60 140 140)")
       (:domain "windowed-scan"  :code "(define (scan-window xs lo hi f) (map f (filter (lambda (x) (and (>= x lo) (<= x hi))) xs)))\n(scan-window '(0 1 2 3 4 5 6) 2 4 (lambda (x) (* x x)))"))
     :explanation "Rung 1: sdf-fill-rect samples the field only within a rectangle, skipping the rest of the canvas. Rung 2: a dirty-rectangle repaint redraws only the screen region that changed, not the whole display. Rung 3: a windowed scan applies a function only to the elements in a range, skipping the rest of the list. All three share one shape — the same per-element operation, but bounded to a sub-region so the work is proportional to the window, not the whole."
     :meta "The transfer works because 'bound the work to the region that matters' is a universal optimization — a spatial or index window over an otherwise-full pass. Dirty-rectangle rendering, viewport culling, and range queries all have this shape. Grounds in book-of-code ch 05 (logic-gates-in-scheme) as the bounded-iteration / windowing shape."
     )
     (:eng "Every windowing substrate shares one gotcha: 'the window must cover everything that should change, or it silently drops work' — the audit is to verify the window contains the region of interest before trusting the speedup." :math "Restriction of a function's evaluation to a sub-domain W ⊆ D; the result agrees with the full pass on W and is undefined (untouched) off W." :cs "The dirty-region / windowed-iteration optimization; the same shape as dirty-rectangle blitting, viewport culling, and range-limited queries."))
  :row-4-proof (
    (:claim "Agreement with sdf-fill on the window: inside a rectangle that covers the shape's bounding box, sdf-fill-rect paints exactly the same pixels sdf-fill would."
     :program "(define (paint? d) (<= d 0))\n(define (in-rect? x y x0 y0 x1 y1) (and (>= x x0) (<= x x1) (>= y y0) (<= y y1)))\n;; inside a covering window, the rect-fill decision equals the full-fill decision\n(define d -2)  ;; a pixel inside the shape\n(display (eq? (paint? d) (and (in-rect? 100 100 60 60 140 140) (paint? d)))) (newline)\n;; a pixel outside the shape but inside the window: both agree not to paint\n(define d2 3)\n(display (eq? (paint? d2) (and (in-rect? 100 100 60 60 140 140) (paint? d2))))"
     :eng-program "(set-mode 'sd)\n(define (both-fills-run? cx cy r color)\n  ;; both the full fill and the bounding-box rect-fill run clean on the same field\n  (let ((field (sdf/circle cx cy r)))\n    (sdf-fill field color)\n    (sdf-fill-rect field (- cx r) (- cy r) (+ cx r) (+ cy r) color)\n    (sdf? field)))\n(both-fills-run? 160 120 30 12)"
     :math-program "(define (covers-bbox? x0 y0 x1 y1 cx cy r)\n  ;; the window covers the circle's bounding box iff it contains [cx-r,cx+r]x[cy-r,cy+r]\n  (and (<= x0 (- cx r)) (>= x1 (+ cx r)) (<= y0 (- cy r)) (>= y1 (+ cy r))))\n(list (covers-bbox? 60 60 140 140 100 100 30)\n      (covers-bbox? 90 90 110 110 100 100 30))"
     :cs-program "(define (agrees-on-window? d in-window?)\n  ;; painted-by-rect = in-window AND painted-by-full; equal to full-fill when in-window\n  (eq? (and in-window? (<= d 0))\n       (if in-window? (<= d 0) #f)))\n(and (agrees-on-window? -2 #t) (agrees-on-window? 3 #t) (agrees-on-window? -2 #f))"
     :invariant "∀ field. ∀ window ⊇ bbox(field). ∀ pixel p ∈ window. paint_rect(p) = paint_full(p)   (a covering window makes rect-fill agree with full-fill on the shape)"
     :meta "The CS pattern is that bounding is a sound optimization ONLY when the window covers the shape: rect-fill = full-fill ∧ in-window, which equals full-fill exactly where the window contains everything the shape touches. Falsified if a shape painted differently under a bounding-box window than under a full fill — that would mean the window clipped part of the shape, i.e. it did not actually cover the bounding box. book-of-code ch 05 (logic-gates-in-scheme) grounds the AND-with-a-window mask; book-of-math ch-advanced-06 (analytic-geometry) grounds the bounding box as a superset of the shape's support."
     )
    (:eng "The engineering consequence is that swapping sdf-fill for a bounding-box sdf-fill-rect is a safe, invisible speedup — the picture is identical, only the cost drops — provided the window covers the shape, which is why deriving the window from the bounding box is the disciplined move." :math "rect-fill restricted to a covering window equals full-fill on that window; off the window both leave pixels untouched, so the images agree." :cs "A correctness-preserving windowing optimization; the same shape as a dirty-rectangle redraw that is pixel-identical to a full redraw when the dirty region covers all changes."))
  :row-5-emergence (

    (:base "sdf-fill-rect paints one field within one bounded window."
     :composition "(set-mode 'sd)\n;; a row of bounded gauges, each painted only in its own bounding box\n(define (gauge cx cy)\n  (sdf-fill-rect (sdf-subtract (sdf/circle cx cy 16) (sdf/circle cx cy 7))\n                 (- cx 18) (- cy 18) (+ cx 18) (+ cy 18) 11))\n(for-each gauge (list 70 130 190 250))"
     :eng-composition "(set-mode 'sd)\n(define (sprite-field cx cy)\n  (sdf-union (sdf/circle cx cy 12) (sdf/box cx (+ cy 10) 8 6)))\n(define (blit-sprite cx cy color)\n  ;; operator build: repaint only the sprite's bounding box each call\n  (sdf-fill-rect (sprite-field cx cy) (- cx 16) (- cy 16) (+ cx 16) (+ cy 20) color))\n(blit-sprite 100 100 12)\n(blit-sprite 200 100 12)"
     :math-composition "(set-mode 'sd)\n;; tile the canvas: paint each cell's field bounded to that cell's rectangle\n(define (tile i j)\n  (let ((cx (+ 60 (* i 100))) (cy (+ 60 (* j 80))))\n    (sdf-fill-rect (sdf/circle cx cy 22) (- cx 26) (- cy 26) (+ cx 26) (+ cy 26)\n                   (if (even? (+ i j)) 12 3))))\n(for-each (lambda (i) (for-each (lambda (j) (tile i j)) (iota 2))) (iota 3))"
     :cs-composition "(set-mode 'sd)\n;; a per-item bounded-paint loop: cost scales with items, not canvas-per-item\n(define items (list (list 80 120 20 12) (list 160 120 24 3) (list 240 120 18 11)))\n(for-each (lambda (it)\n            (let ((cx (car it)) (cy (cadr it)) (r (caddr it)) (col (cadddr it)))\n              (sdf-fill-rect (sdf/circle cx cy r) (- cx r) (- cy r) (+ cx r) (+ cy r) col)))\n          items)"
     :emergent "A dashboard of gauges, a set of sprites, a checkerboard of tiles: each element is painted only within its own bounding box, so a scene of many small shapes costs the sum of their areas, not the canvas times their count — the sprite-blit / dirty-rectangle pattern."
     :meta "This is the bounded-rendering / sprite-blit family — many small fills each clipped to their own region so total cost tracks the shapes, not the canvas, distinct from CSG (which builds each field) and from painter's-algorithm layering (which orders full fills). Familiar analogs are sprite blitting in a 2-D engine, dirty-rectangle screen updates, and tile-based renderers. The composition works because a bounding-box window makes each rect-fill agree with a full fill (Row 4), so a loop of bounded paints is a correct scene at a fraction of the sampling cost. Grounds in book-of-code ch 05 (logic-gates-in-scheme) as the per-region-bounded-iteration shape."
     )
    (:eng "The audit lens is 'total cost = sum of window areas, not canvas × count' — the SRE win for a 12-gauge dashboard is repainting twelve small boxes per frame instead of twelve full canvases, and each window must still cover its shape or that gauge clips." :math "A scene of bounded fills is the union of per-window rasterizations; total work is Σ area(window_i), and each window must contain its shape's bounding box for correctness." :cs "Sprite-blit / dirty-rectangle loop; the same shape as a 2-D engine repainting only the changed tiles each frame."))
)
